                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.34",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The delay test no longer hangs on its spinner — or counts past the number of configs",
                            "Three separate defects that looked like one thing:\n\n**1) A timeout that could never fire.** The delay measurement is a direct call into the core, and the timeout wrapped around it was the kind that **only acts at a suspension point** — and a call into the core is not one. Measured on the device: three tests started and **returned nothing for over three minutes**, on a 12-second timeout. Nothing after it ever ran, so the list never filled and the spinner never stopped. It now runs on a thread that can be abandoned.\n\n**2) A time budget shorter than the handshake itself.** One handshake through the SNI engine takes **9 to 14 seconds** on an Iranian mobile line — confirmed by a `101` straight from the worker, on all four routes tried. A test budgeted at 7 to 12 seconds always said \"no ping\", not because the config was broken but because time ran out. It is now 25 seconds, each config gets a second attempt, and they run two at a time instead of three — they all pass through one engine, and crowding it spoils the reading.\n\n**3) A shared counter.** The second measuring stage was a copy of the first and shared its counter, so eight configs **counted as far as 16**, and it exited by a route that skipped the line clearing the spinner. It is now one function that makes its own counter every time, and the spinner is cleared somewhere **no exit can skip**.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "SNI bypass: 14 connection methods, measured and chosen for your line",
                            "The entry point was right and the config was right, and still not one config would ping on one Iranian mobile network. The cause was elsewhere: **the method that hides the TLS handshake** was a constant in the app, and the engine actually has five levers, not one.\n\nMeasured on that line, three attempts each, against one edge under one forged name:\n\n• the method that shipped until today — **zero bytes, all three times**\n• chunk fragmentation — **answered all three, 1.2 to 2 seconds**\n• TLS-record fragmentation — **answered all three**\n• and for reference, no engine at all also answered all three — so the line was healthy and the method was the problem\n\nThere are now **14 methods** — every combination of fragment size, inter-fragment delay, the TTL trick and the injection mode — and the measure button walks them one at a time until it finds the first that answers on your line, then keeps it. The method that already worked stays **first in the list**, so a line that was connecting before is tested first and is no slower.\n\nTo keep it quick, methods are probed against only three configs — all that stage has to answer is whether anything comes back at all — and the full list is measured once afterwards, through the winner. **No operator is named anywhere**: what gets chosen comes from a measurement of your own line, not from a guess about your network.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "SNI anti-filter: 28 entry points in 6 methods, up from 8",
                            "An entry point is an edge address plus the name put on the handshake, and which PAIRS get through is a property of **the network you are on**, not of the app.\n\nThis same build connected on one Iranian mobile operator and not on another, and the reason was plain: the addresses the second one answers on were not in the list at all.\n\nThere are 28 now, grouped into six \"methods\" shown under each row. **No operator is named anywhere in the app** — it would be a guess about your network that goes stale the week that operator changes what it blocks. The right question is asked instead: all of them are measured, and the fastest that answered is taken.",
                            Icons.Default.Security
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Iran configs: release 50, and every combination",
                            "These configs have no server: they ask an encrypted DNS resolver for every name and fragment the TLS handshake. So **two** things decide whether one works on a given line, and they fail independently.\n\nMeasured on an Iranian mobile line: the upstream project's own default resolver timed out after 12 s on every name — meaning **no name resolved and no site opened**, which from outside looks exactly like \"the config does not work\". Elsewhere the fragmentation itself closed sites that were open: with it, www.cloudflare.com gave nothing in 22 s; without it, 3.9 s.\n\nThere are 19 configs now: four fragment shapes (v50 fragA/fragB and v48) across three resolvers, plus three \"clean DNS only\" variants. The same files the Windows app uses, from one shared generator, so the two can never drift apart.",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A \"test these on your connection\" button",
                            "On the Iran configs screen. It asks each config whether its DNS resolver answers from your connection, and writes the result under that row.\n\nThat is the decisive half: if the resolver cannot be reached, the config **cannot** work and trying it is wasted time. Each resolver is measured once for its whole group, so nineteen rows are answered by four requests.\n\nThe other half — whether the fragmentation gets past your operator's DPI — cannot be answered without connecting, and is not claimed: a green row means \"stands a chance\", not \"will work\".",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.33",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The combine assistant now finishes the job",
                            "«ترکیب» in Config Studio armed the scanner and then abandoned you on it: the base config was filled in, the subscriber was pre-selected in the combine sheet, and that was all.\n\nThe assistant that walks exactly this job from «ابری» was **never started** for a Studio subscriber — and it could not have worked if it had, because it resolved its source only from cloud groups and a Studio subscriber is not one.\n\nIt now travels with the handover and does the whole of it. All that is left to you is **what the scan optimises for and how many clean addresses to find**. After that it builds the combinations, measures every one with a real request, and **writes the ones that answered into that person's subscription**.\n\nIt names the subscriber on every step, because an operator fixing several people in a sitting is one mis-click from publishing to the wrong one.\n\nA failed publish stops the guide on an orange bar and leaves «بروزرسانی ساب» on the group's card to retry. And a Studio combine no longer copies the subscriber's configs into your own connection list — that step is right for a cloud combine and wrong for one done on somebody else's behalf.",
                            Icons.Default.AutoAwesome
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The link on the subscriber's page is a subscription again",
                            "Under «لینک اشتراک», the page printed **its own address**.\n\nAnyone who reached that page by opening their link in a browser — the ordinary way a person opens a link they were sent — was shown exactly what was already in their address bar. So the section that exists to hand over a subscription handed over the status page, and the obvious conclusion is that no subscription link was ever given.\n\nIt now prints the link with `?format=base64`, which is read **before** the browser-versus-client guess and forces the server list. So the link the page offers imports whoever fetches it and however they do it — including clients that fetch through a WebView with a borrowed browser user agent, which until now were answered with a page of HTML where their parser expected servers.\n\nThe one-tap app buttons carry the same link.\n\n**Redeploy the engine on your accounts to pick this up** (Config Studio → account → update engine). Build 14.",
                            Icons.Default.Link
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "«قالب» — a config shape, named",
                            "The `templates` table has existed since the first Config Studio migration and **not one row was ever written into it**.\n\nA «بسته» is what somebody is *allowed* — volume, days, caps. A «قالب» is what their link *looks like* — protocol, transport, handshake, which endpoints.\n\nMerging them would mean a new row every time either half changed, and picking from twenty combinations of four things.\n\n**A template is a starting point, never a live reference.** A config copies the shape when it is made and carries it afterwards — so editing one changes nothing for anybody already holding a link, and there is deliberately no Apply button: re-shaping an existing config means re-issuing its credential.\n\nFour starting points, each setting something actually different on the wire: **Steady**, **Phone**, **Game**, and **Upgrades blocked**.\n\nGame is the same shape as Steady and says so: nothing in a link makes a connection faster, so a \"gaming protocol\" would be a label over nothing. What it is for is the endpoint choice.",
                            Icons.Default.Layers
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A config can be served on chosen endpoints",
                            "Until now every config rendered on **every** enabled endpoint. A shape can name its endpoints now, and a config built from it carries that.\n\nIf an endpoint it names no longer exists, the config falls back to **all** of them rather than to none — a config that renders zero links is a subscription entry that vanishes, and \"the endpoint was deleted\" is not something the person holding the link can see or act on.",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Plans and shapes are finally connected",
                            "`Plan.templateId` has been in the schema, on the wire and in the model since the beginning, and **nothing had ever set it** — every plan ever made here carried a null.\n\nThe plan editor picks a shape now, and creating somebody on that plan — one at a time or five hundred at a time — builds their config from it.\n\nThe builder gained both directions: **start from a stored shape**, or **keep what you just built** as one.\n\nAnd in bulk: **give a whole selection a config** from a shape — added, never replacing, so every existing link keeps working and one more server appears in their subscription.",
                            Icons.Default.Inventory2
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "One thing, done to many people",
                            "**Select** in the user list turns on checkmarks. The selection then goes to a page of its own: switch on or off, renew or take back, the three caps, fetch every link, delete.\n\n**Every action reports per person, never as one verdict.** Nineteen of twenty succeeding is the ordinary outcome across a fleet — an account unreachable, a name deleted from another device — and a single \"failed\" would hide which nineteen worked.\n\nIt does not stop at the first failure, and it is sequential rather than concurrent: twenty parallel requests to one worker is twenty concurrent D1 transactions on a free plan, to save a second.\n\nThe loop is on the client because **a selection is not a shard**: twenty people chosen off one list can live on twenty different Cloudflare accounts.\n\nThe selection survives a change of filter, so you can narrow by tag, select all, and act on that.",
                            Icons.Default.Rule
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Many users at once, from a prefix and a count",
                            "Prefix, count, starting number, a «بسته», volume, days and a device cap — and a list of links at the end.\n\n**A preview on every keystroke**: \"mlm-001 … mlm-020 — 20 people\". That is what catches a mistyped prefix and a count with an extra zero. A name already taken anywhere in the fleet is flagged while typing too — not nineteen creations in.\n\nNumbers are zero-padded to one width so the list stays in order wherever it is pasted.\n\nEach person costs the same three calls one person does, in the same order: create, attach a config, then read the link back. A subscription with no config resolves to an **empty** document — HTTP 200 with zero servers — which imports cleanly and never connects. So somebody whose config failed is reported by **name** instead of being handed a link.\n\nAt the end: copy the whole list, share it, save it as a file, or open one QR at a time. At most 500 a run — a guard against a slipped digit, not a technical ceiling.",
                            Icons.Default.GroupAdd
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Handing somebody their link without the clipboard",
                            "Copy was the only way out of the user page, which works when you are already standing in the chat you mean to paste into and not otherwise.\n\nThree more ways now: a **QR code** for a phone next to you, the **Android share sheet** for whatever you have installed, and **save to a file**.\n\nWhen the link is larger than a QR can hold — a subscription carrying a config per endpoint per port is past three kilobytes — the card says why there is none rather than drawing a square that scans into nothing.\n\nThe file is written into the cache and never left in Downloads: a file full of live subscription links is a copy nobody remembers to delete.",
                            Icons.Default.QrCode
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Tags, a private note, and a list that can finally be ordered",
                            "`note` and `tags` have existed since the first Config Studio migration and **nothing had ever written to either**. They are editable on the user page now. Neither appears on the subscriber\'s own page or reaches the client that reads the link.\n\nTags become the filter row above the user list. Beside them: **six orders** (newest, oldest, by name, expiring soonest, heaviest use, last connected) and **seven filters**.\n\nThree of those filters are conditions the engine never writes into a column: expiry is evaluated when somebody connects, so reading \"expired\" off the status column would draw an empty list on an installation full of expired people.\n\nThe search no longer covers the name alone — name, tag, note and id together — and a count above the list says how many the narrowing matched.",
                            Icons.Default.Label
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A renewal can be taken back",
                            "Until now a renewal could only **give**. Type 300 where 30 was meant and the only way back was deleting the person — which loses their link, the one thing the whole renewal flow exists to protect.\n\nThere is a **take back** direction now, with two hard floors: the expiry will not be pushed before this moment, and the volume will not drop below what has already been used (a quota under the meter refuses them forever, including after the next top-up). A reduction does not switch a stopped subscriber back on.\n\nAnd **zeroing the usage meter** is its own action: only the meter moves, the expiry and quota stay — for when the volume was spent by something that was not them.\n\nOn an account whose engine has not been updated, neither control is drawn: the old engine takes a negative figure unclamped and reads the new mode as an ordinary renewal, and both failures are silent.",
                            Icons.Default.RemoveCircleOutline
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Device and connection limits are enforced now, not merely recorded",
                            "Until now those numbers were stored, displayed, and **never refused anyone** — and every screen in the app said so honestly. They are real now.\n\nA cap is only a cap if counting and admitting happen in **one indivisible step**. Everything simpler fails under exactly the load the cap exists to limit: with KV, two connections in two data centres both read zero and both get in. A database table works and eats the write budget — one person connected for eight hours is about 480 writes, and twelve such people finish the day's allowance.\n\nSo: a **Durable Object per user**. Single-threaded, so that indivisible step is free, and its own storage does not touch the database budget at all.\n\nA few decisions inside it that matter:\n\n- **A device already seen does not take a new slot.** Otherwise \"one device\" means \"one connection, ever\".\n- **Two connections from one address are one address.**\n- **No raw address is ever stored** — only a salted, truncated hash. Enough to count distinct addresses; not enough to leave a list of subscriber addresses sitting on somebody's Cloudflare account.\n- **Device recognition is an estimate and says so everywhere it appears.** Two phones behind one router can count as one.\n\nAnd a **per-person switch**: limits can be counted and shown without refusing anyone, for the case where you want to watch before you cut off.\n\nOn an account with no Durable Object everything falls back to what it did before: counted, shown, not enforced. If a deploy cannot create the object it **deploys without it** — taking someone's tunnel down to add a limit is worse than the limit going unapplied.",
                            Icons.Default.Devices
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A renewed person was refused by the tunnel immediately",
                            "This one was live on real accounts, and no screen could see it.\n\nThe legacy model counted expiry **from the creation date**. The new one holds an absolute date and keeps the old column as a mirror so a rolled-back engine still enforces something. But renewing wrote that mirror as *days remaining from now*, while the tunnel read it as *days from creation*.\n\nSo someone 29 days into a 30-day term who renewed for 15 more got a mirror saying 16 — meaning \"expired sixteen days after they were created\", which was two weeks ago. The tunnel refused them on the spot while every screen showed them fifteen days of credit.\n\nThe tunnel reads the absolute date now, and the old column is only a fallback.",
                            Icons.Default.EventBusy
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Every usage figure was frozen on the day the account was upgraded",
                            "The limits themselves worked — the tunnel reads them from the legacy columns and those were being updated. What did not work was **every number you or your user could see**.\n\nThe byte columns, which the migration filled in once, were never added to again. And those are the ones the subscription header sends to the person's own client, the ones their page draws its bar from, the ones the user list shows, and the ones the \"over quota\" tile counts. Someone 25 GB into 30 was shown as having used nothing.\n\nBoth families now move in the **same statement** — no extra database write.\n\nThe 24-hour traffic tile had the same shape of problem: it summed a table nothing wrote, so it always read zero. It is written now, but **coalesced in the worker and sent about once per 500 MB** — that column feeds a chart rather than a limit, and the write budget is needed elsewhere.",
                            Icons.Default.QueryStats
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"Resets every month\" now resets, and \"time starts on first connection\" now starts",
                            "The volume reset policy was a column you could set, the subscriber page could describe, and **nothing acted on**. Volume comes back at the end of a period now — and the next boundary advances from the **previous** one rather than from today, so someone who does not connect for two months lands back on the same day of the cycle instead of dragging their own renewal date along with them.\n\nAnd a term that starts on first connection now does: until the link is used there is no end date at all, because a link sitting unopened should not burn its own time. The first connection starts the clock, once in that person's life.",
                            Icons.Default.RestartAlt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A path of its own for each config, without the worker announcing itself",
                            "WebSocket was answered only on `/`, so a per-person path was impossible.\n\nThe obvious fix — accept an upgrade on any unreserved path — is worse than the problem: it answers 101 to an upgrade aimed anywhere, and the camouflage page exists precisely so that everything this worker does not serve looks like an ordinary web server.\n\nSo a path no config has registered still gets the nginx page. Only a path a config **actually claims** is a tunnel, and deleting a config gives its path back. The lookup runs **only for paths other than `/`**, so the common case costs no extra read at all.",
                            Icons.Default.AltRoute
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Config Studio — create people, hand out a subscription link, control volume and time, on your own Cloudflare account",
                            "A whole product inside this app. It installs on **your** Cloudflare account, you create people, each gets a subscription link, and you control how much volume and how long it lasts. None of it passes through us.\n\n**It installs, or it adopts what is already there.** The account is examined first and the offer depends on what was found. Installing from a second phone now **finds the existing installation** instead of minting a second worker beside it — which is what it used to do, and that second worker answered on the old address, was never upgraded again, and was the target of every link handed out from the first phone. The worker's name is now derived from the account itself, so any device asks for the same one.\n\nAnd when it **cannot see the account**, there is no install button at all. Not being able to see an account is precisely the state in which installing does damage.\n\n**The subscription link** carries the headers every mainstream client reads, so volume and expiry appear in the person's own app without them opening anything. There is also a **Persian, right-to-left page**: volume left, the end date, and if they have been cut off, **why**, in a sentence. No number is drawn on that page that this installation cannot actually measure.\n\n**Renewing does not change the link.** That is the most important property of the whole flow: the person holding it does nothing, installs nothing, and does not have to be contacted. Renewing early is not punished either — the clock moves from the current expiry, not from today.\n\nRotating a token kills the old one **immediately**. A link you believe you revoked must not still work.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Plans — write the terms once, hand them to twenty people",
                            "A plan like \"30 GB / 30 days\" is written once and then chosen when you add someone.\n\nChoosing one **fills the fields rather than hiding them** — every number is still yours to change before you create anyone. Daily volume, concurrent connection and IP limits, the reset policy, and whether the clock starts when they are added or on their first connection all live on the plan itself.\n\n**Editing a plan changes nothing for the people already on it.** That is deliberate and it is the safe default: somebody holding a link should not wake up on different terms. Pushing an edit onto them is a separate, confirmed action — and even that **leaves volume and expiry alone**, because someone topped up by a renewal last week must not be pulled back down by an operator who only meant to change a device limit.\n\nA plan is written to **every** account. If one is unreachable at that moment, the plan says which account does not have it — drift is visible rather than sitting quietly until somebody is created on the wrong terms.\n\nThere is no money anywhere in this product — no word for it and no column for it — and a build-time guard checks that over the strings **and** over the worker's code and database schema.",
                            Icons.Default.Inventory2
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Many Cloudflare accounts, with no cap — automatic placement and a capacity meter per account",
                            "Every Cloudflare ceiling is **per account**: a hundred thousand requests a day, a hundred thousand database writes. A second account does not make the first one bigger, it adds another full set. So there is an accounts list now, and no limit on how many.\n\n**Placement is automatic, not an option.** Nobody picks by hand out of fifty accounts. The emptiest healthy account wins; anything unreachable or on an older engine is skipped; and an account whose load has never been read **does not win by default** — \"unknown\" and \"empty\" are the same number and only one of them is a reason to send everyone there. The choice is shown **before** it is committed, never after: it decides which worker serves that person's link.\n\n**Add several** connects a pasted list of accounts in one pass. Each one runs the same account scan, so an account that already has an installation is adopted rather than given a second worker — pasting one twice is safe. And one failure is one row, not the end of the queue.\n\n**A capacity meter** per account and in total, and it says plainly that it is **an estimate rather than a reading from Cloudflare**: the request budget cannot be read from inside the worker, and counting requests in the database would spend the very budget it was measuring.\n\nEverything that acts on one account moved onto that account's own page. \"Repair the engine\" used to repair whichever account sorted first while reading as though it were about the installation in general.",
                            Icons.Default.Cloud
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The sync was silently dropping people",
                            "The user list is read from a local copy — with an uncapped number of accounts, a list that asked each one for a page would be one request per account per screen.\n\nThat copy was brought up to date with a timestamp watermark: I have read this far, give me what changed after it. But when a page ended **inside a group of rows sharing one timestamp**, the rest of that group was skipped **permanently** — the watermark had already moved past them.\n\nAnd those ties were not exotic. Applying a plan to its users writes one identical timestamp across all of them, and the original migration gave every legacy user the same one. So on a busy account the list was already incomplete and **looked complete**, which is the worst direction for this to fail in.\n\nThe watermark now carries a second half saying where inside that group the last page stopped, with an index in exactly that shape so reading it does not cost the whole table. An engine that has not been updated keeps its old behaviour, and the app notices it is not advancing and says to update that account rather than re-reading the same page fifty times.",
                            Icons.Default.Sync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Someone created from a plan did not get the plan's terms",
                            "Only three things travelled: volume, duration and the device limit. **Daily volume, the concurrent connection limit, the IP limit, the reset policy, and whether the clock starts on first connection** — all five were silently dropped, even though the engine stores every one of them and the screen said the plan \"fills the fields\".\n\nThe effect was that \"apply to the people on this plan\" appeared to be re-applying terms that had never been applied even once. A new person is now built from the plan itself, with only the three numbers actually on screen written over it.\n\nAnd the **plan editor had no field for those five at all** — so every plan ever made in this app had them empty. Since \"apply\" includes them by default, pressing it wrote those blanks over people who may have had them set by hand. All five are on the screen now.",
                            Icons.Default.Rule
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A deleted person's name was burned forever",
                            "A delete here is a tombstone rather than an erase, because that is the only thing that tells another device the person is gone. But usernames are unique and the tombstone kept holding the name: a name used once could never be used again.\n\nWorse, it was **silent**. The local list drops tombstones, so the app said \"that name is free\", you typed it, and the engine refused. A tombstone now gives up its name and stays a tombstone. A name held by a **live** person is still refused, and still says so.\n\nOne more from the same family: reading a deleted person returned success. That is the request made before every change, so a deleted user could be painted on screen as though they were live.",
                            Icons.Default.PersonRemove
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A contact line on the subscriber's page, and some security housekeeping",
                            "The subscriber page was always meant to show your contact line when someone's subscription runs out — and **there was no way to write it**. It is set on that account's page now, in whatever words you choose, and the app ships none of its own. Same for the name at the top of the page. These settings are a fixed list rather than a free key/value store: otherwise the same path could have overwritten the legacy panel password or a spent install secret.\n\n**Every \"update engine\" or \"repair\" minted a fresh access key and never revoked the old one.** The app kept only the newest while the engine went on honouring all of them; ten repairs left ten live admin keys. The previous key for that device is now revoked once the new one is proven to work.\n\nAnd the account scan no longer reports a worker it **could not inspect** as \"nothing here\" — that answer made the wizard offer to install, which means adding a second worker beside one we had merely failed to look at.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The connect bar is alive in all three states, not only while connecting",
                            "The soft colour drifting behind the connect bar’s glass **only moved when it was amber**. Green and red were paint.\n\nThat was deliberate, and for a good reason: the drift ran on `rememberInfiniteTransition`, which asks the window for **a frame every vsync** for as long as it is composed — whatever its value does, and even if nobody reads it. This bar is on screen the entire time the config list is: 371 frames in six idle seconds, on the one screen that also has to scroll hundreds of rows. The answer then was to freeze the movement unless the tunnel was coming up.\n\nBut green and red are the states the bar sits in for **hours**, and a wash that does not move in them is a gradient painted on the glass rather than light behind it — which is the whole difference the wash exists to make. So this time the **clock was thrown away, not the movement**: it advances on `delay` (which requests no frame at all, it only writes a float), it is read in the **draw** phase (so a tick re-records one display list and recomposes nothing), and it ticks every 64ms instead of every vsync — **about a quarter of the frames**.\n\nThat rate is affordable because one cycle of this wash is **three minutes**. Replaying the same noise over the real geometry: a light’s centre moves about 4px between ticks and 20px at its steepest, across a radial gradient whose falloff is 542px wide and which has no edge anywhere.\n\n**A three-minute jump was found and fixed on the way.** The clock returned to zero at the end of each cycle, but the noise behind the drift has no period — so every three minutes all four lights **teleported 617px at once**. It had always done that, and had never been seen, because the movement only ever ran for the few seconds a tunnel takes to come up. Running always, it would have been.",
                            Icons.Default.Gradient
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "About moved into Settings, as the last row of the About group",
                            "Its icon is **gone from the home screen**. A tile there said this was a destination on the level of a transport, when it is the one screen nobody opens twice — and it was holding a slot on the grid.\n\nIt is **Settings › About › About Us** now: the last row of the same group that already carries the version, the updater and the crash reports, because that group is already the answer to every question this screen answers. Last rather than first, because the three rows above it are things you come to Settings to DO; this one is reading.\n\nAnd on a tablet or a television it **splits into two panes like the rest of Settings**: About and the changelog open in the detail pane while the settings list stays put beside them. That did not come for free — the changelog is no longer a flag held inside the About screen, it is a route on the settings stack, so physical back walks Changelog → About → Settings through the one stack that owns the rest of the tree.\n\nAn icon layout you arranged yourself is left alone; only this one icon drops out of it, and it leaves no gap.",
                            Icons.Default.Groups
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Iran configs and domain fronting, each with an icon on the home screen",
                            "Both were folders inside the config list \u2014 and that list is where configs **arrive**: a panel deployed, a subscription imported, a scan combined. These two are the opposite. They ship with the app, there is nothing to deploy, and neither has anything to do with the Cloudflare account or the panels the rest of that screen is about.\n\nAnd someone who needs the Iran defaults needs them on the day nothing else works \u2014 \u201copen the config list and find the right folder among the panels\u201d is not a path anyone walks in that state.\n\nEach has its own icon now and **connects from there**: one tap on a row, tap again to disconnect. Domain fronting\u2019s full setup wizard is on its screen too, and **above** the list rather than beside it, because the order is not cosmetic \u2014 its config cannot carry anything until the certificate it terminates TLS with is one this device trusts.\n\nThe lamp for each lights on its own icon rather than on V2Ray, which is where it used to go: anyone who tapped that found a list with the connected config hidden in a folder inside it.\n\nAnd they are **gone from the config list**. Two places showing one thing are two places to keep in step, and one more list to search when what you want is somewhere else. The configs themselves are untouched and still connectable \u2014 just from their own screen.",
                            Icons.Default.Flag
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The test counter no longer counts more than you can see",
                            "One config in the list and delay said two; three configs and it said four.\n\nEach test rebuilt its own target list from the full store, with its own copy of the panel/folder rule \u2014 and that store still holds the SNI configs, which this screen deliberately hides. So the extras were the hidden SNI copies of the same panel, being measured where nobody could see the result, and over a path that cannot work for them anyway because the SNI engine is not in the way.\n\nAll three tests now work from exactly the list the screen is showing, so the count, the rows and the work cannot disagree.",
                            Icons.Default.Numbers
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The config screen: readings one at a time, no jump, and two jump arrows",
                            "**Readings no longer wait for the end of the run.** Each one was published by REPLACING the whole list \u2014 and this screen derives a great deal from that list: it filters the SNI configs out (parsing every config\u2019s URI to decide), sorts the rest through a comparator, then filters again by panel and folder. At three hundred configs that is **a hundred thousand URI parses and three hundred sorts** over one run, on the main thread, so the frames the results were meant to appear in never got drawn and everything landed at once at the end. Readings now go into a live map that the ROW reads, so one number invalidates one row; the list itself is written once, when the run finishes. It is what the SNI list already did, which is why that one was never slow.\n\n**It no longer jumps into the middle.** Pressing delay marks every row `\u2026`, and `\u2026` is not a reading \u2014 so the sort key of the whole list vanished in one frame and it reshuffled, carrying the viewport with it. The order is frozen while a run is in flight and the list goes to the top; it sorts once at the end. The sort button takes you to the top too.\n\n**Two jump arrows.** Scroll down and a down arrow offers the end of the list; scroll up and an up arrow offers the top. Direction comes from a single position value rather than comparing the index and the offset separately \u2014 the offset restarts at zero whenever a new row appears, which reported \u201cup\u201d mid-scroll-down. And the jump covers the distance at once and animates only the ARRIVAL, because `animateScrollToItem` over three hundred rows does not animate the journey: it approximates and corrects, which arrives as the list lurching forward in blocks.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The app no longer redraws itself for nothing",
                            "The connect card\u2019s colour wash asked for **a new frame every vsync for as long as it was on screen**, forever \u2014 which is exactly what this app\u2019s own code comments warn about for that kind of animation. Measured on the device, six idle seconds on the config screen: **371 frames before, 0 after**.\n\nThe colour is always there; the movement now happens only while connecting, which is the state where movement means something. Red and green are states, and a state does not need to shimmer to be read.",
                            Icons.Default.BatteryChargingFull
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Every reading appears as it is taken, not at the end",
                            "On the SNI list and on the config screen \u2014 two separate causes.\n\nOn the SNI list the results were collected until the last config and written in one go; each is published the moment it is known now. On the config screen the numbers **were arriving and being wiped**: the screen re-seeded itself from disk every time anything saved, and a bulk delay test saves as it goes, so every row still showing \u201c\u2026\u201d was reset to whatever the disk last held. Membership comes from the store now and the measurements from the screen, which is the only place that knows them yet.\n\nThe SNI list also **re-orders while it runs**, fastest climbing to the top. That was wrong once too: the order was held in a cache whose key never changed, so the numbers arrived and the list sat still.",
                            Icons.Default.Timeline
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Swipe a row aside to delete it, like a call list",
                            "Deleting **one** thing belongs on the thing, not in a toolbar: a toolbar button has to act on \u201cthe selected one\u201d, so deleting means selecting first \u2014 and selecting is also how you choose what to connect.\n\nThree things it got wrong first. The row was moved with an offset, so it kept its full width and drew over the bin; it is narrowed now, and the bin takes exactly the space it leaves. The name slid the wrong way \u2014 in a right-to-left layout the bin belongs on the physical right, so the child order is chosen from the layout direction. And it stuttered, because every finger position was a fresh animation target; it now tracks the finger one to one and only animates when the finger lifts, on a spring with no bounce, so it settles the same whether it opens or thinks better of it.",
                            Icons.Default.SwipeLeft
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The SNI config list wears the connection screen\u2019s toolbar",
                            "Delay, sort, delete, delete-the-dead and delete-all \u2014 at the top of the page, exactly where the config screen keeps them. This is a list of configs, and everything anyone wants to do with a list of configs is those three things: measure it, order it, take something out of it. It used to be a list you could only pick from.\n\nSorting puts the fastest first, then the ones that answered nothing, then the unmeasured \u2014 a config with no reading is not a slow config, it has not been asked. Delete-the-dead takes only configs that were measured and did not answer, never ones whose turn has not come.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "One button for both halves of the SNI measurement",
                            "\u201cMeasure and pick the fastest\u201d now measures the **entry points** first and adopts the fastest that answered, then measures the **configs** through that entry point.\n\nThey were two separate buttons, and that put the burden of knowing the order on the user: measuring configs over a slow entry point measures the entry point, and adopting a faster one afterwards throws every number away. One press does both now, in the only order in which either answer means anything.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Two buttons left the connection screen",
                            "The **bulk download-speed test** is gone. It pulled a real download through every config in a list that is routinely hundreds long \u2014 minutes of traffic to answer a question the delay test beside it already answers well enough to sort by. The per-config version is still on the config\u2019s own page, which is where asking about **one** server belongs.\n\nAnd **building SNI configs has left the + menu**. It lives in the SNI section, which is where the engine, the list and the connection all are; two doors into one operation are two places that have to be kept in step forever.",
                            Icons.Default.RemoveCircleOutline
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Configs no longer delete themselves \u2014 and the app\u2019s most common crash is gone",
                            "180 SNI configs were built; on coming back to the app they had all vanished. The cause is the same one behind the crash that dominates the reports: twelve places in the app recorded a small change by **wiping the whole stored list and writing their own copy over it**.\n\nTwo failures came out of that. A test that runs for minutes knows something about the configs it measured and nothing about the rest, so writing its result as a whole-list replacement erased anything added while it ran. And clearing then refilling is two operations rather than one: two of them overlapping left the list holding **two copies of every config with the same id** \u2014 which is exactly the error that closed the config screen, `Key \u2026 was already used`. Across 1.2.31 and 1.2.32 it was the single most reported crash.\n\nEvery write now merges by id, touching only the rows that actually changed; deletions go by id too, and both happen under one lock. A second guard was added underneath: a duplicate id can no longer be written at all \u2014 it used to be written and then quietly dropped on the next load, taking the second copy with it.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The shared list no longer goes down mid-day",
                            "Yesterday the database hit its daily ceiling and the shared config list stopped answering for everyone. One clause did it: the trial configs were picked with `ORDER BY RANDOM()`, and RANDOM cannot use an index — returning 6 rows meant reading every row for that network, and since nothing was ever deleted, that cost grew every day.\n\nThe sample is now taken from a random point on the hash ring instead. The hash is already uniform, so it gives the same randomness for the price of exactly the rows it returns. Measured on a 60,000-row table: **378,760 steps down to 111** — and unlike the old one, that number does not grow with the table. Every other query on the serving path was given a ceiling too, a sweep now drops dead rows (nothing was ever deleted before), and the stats dashboard stopped rescanning the whole table on every refresh.",
                            Icons.Default.Storage
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "SNI configs can be deleted",
                            "They could be built and never removed. The list only grew — and because the build step skips a config it has already made, there was not even a way to rebuild one of them cleanly.\n\nThe config picker now carries two actions: delete the selected config, and delete all of them. Both ask a second time, and both take away only the SNI copy — the config it was built from is untouched. Deleting the one that is carrying traffic disconnects first, because otherwise the dial went on saying connected over a config that no longer existed.",
                            Icons.Default.DeleteSweep
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The SNI lamp no longer comes on by itself",
                            "Reported: the SNI light on the home screen sometimes came on while using the tunnel or another engine. It was real, and it was a leak. The SNI engine is a separate process that five places started and none stopped, so a single delay test over a list that happened to contain one SNI config left that process running for the rest of the app’s life — and the home screen read it as the SNI engine being on.\n\nTwo things changed. The engine is no longer switched on: it is borrowed and given back, and it goes down when the last holder lets go — whether the measurement finished, was stopped, or the connection ended. And the lamp no longer watches for a live process; it watches for a config actually riding it. The engine on its own carries nothing. It is a door, and a door nobody is walking through is not a connection.",
                            Icons.Default.Lightbulb
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "SNI is built from cloud configs only",
                            "BPB, EDG, Nahan and MLM — and their combined configs, because a combine only rewrites the endpoint and keeps the panel a config came from.\n\nThis is not a preference. Converting means pointing a config at the local door so that door can dial a Cloudflare edge and let the edge route the rest, so the config’s own server has to be something that edge can reach. A manually added or subscription config usually has a server of its own that Cloudflare has no route to; converting one produced a config that could not connect on any entry point — which reads as SNI being broken rather than as that config never being eligible. Those are no longer offered, and the same rule now applies to the second conversion entry point on the add-config screen, so two doors into one operation stop disagreeing about it.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudQueue
                        ),
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.32",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "An \"unblock configs\" switch in Settings",
                            "For the case where Cloudflare is reachable and nothing goes through it. Turned on, the TLS handshake takes a different shape: the browser fingerprint is dropped and a cipher list with a TLS 1.2 ceiling is sent instead. Dropping the fingerprint is the point rather than an omission — with one set, Xray switches to uTLS, which builds the ClientHello from a stored browser profile and ignores the cipher list entirely. The version ceiling is needed for the same reason: Go does not apply a cipher list to TLS 1.3, so without it the setting would do nothing on any modern server. Off by default, and it should be: the ordinary handshake is the one that looks like everybody else's and this one does not. It is for when that resemblance is the problem.",
                            Icons.Default.Https
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "JSON configs copied out of a chat",
                            "The parser required the text to BE a JSON document, and three things break that, all of which happen when a config is copied from a messaging app: invisible characters that do not count as whitespace (a BOM, the bidi marks a Persian chat inserts — `trim()` removes neither), any prose the user copied along with it, and several pieces stuck together. It now only has to CONTAIN a document: a scanner counts braces and extracts each complete one, tracking strings so a brace inside a value cannot end a document early. And when the text is plainly a JSON config that will not parse, the message says it arrived incomplete and suggests sending it as a file — rather than \"invalid config\", which is a verdict on the config itself.",
                            Icons.Default.DataObject
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The V2Ray connect card is lit from inside",
                            "The bar at the bottom of the V2Ray screen now carries the colour of the connection: green while it is carrying, amber while it comes up, red while it is down. The screen answers “am I connected” before a word has been read.\n\nIt is not a gradient painted on the glass. It is four radial light fields drifting on separate noise tracks, so what is seen is the mix rather than the shapes, and the colour has no edge where it begins -- built from transparency rather than colours mixed toward the page, which is why it works on a dark surface at all. Taken from NetDoctor unchanged, colour for colour.\n\nTwo things came with it. The whole bar is the switch now rather than the button at its end: everything printed on the bar is about the connection that button makes, so a full-width bar with one small target was the wrong shape. And the button’s mark and word are plain ink instead of the state colour -- on a bar that is itself red, amber or green, two colours compete to say one thing and the smaller one wins the eye.",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The V2Ray list no longer ends in a cut",
                            "The server list sits between two fixed things — the filter row above it and the connect card below — and met both with a hard edge: a row was fully drawn at one pixel and gone at the next, which reads as a seam across the screen rather than as content continuing past the frame. The same fade the settings screens use now applies here, dissolving the last few dp so a row leaving the viewport thins out into the surface it is passing under. The bottom fade is longer than the top on purpose: rows leave downward under a card that is itself opaque, and a short fade there still leaves a visible line where the two meet.",
                            Icons.Default.ViewList
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A real delay test for every Psiphon country",
                            "The country list has a “measure every country” row at the top now. It connects to each one in turn, times a real request through the tunnel, then orders the list fastest first and adopts the winner. The measurement goes over Psiphon’s local SOCKS port, because the app excludes its own package from its own tun -- an ordinary socket would ride the carrier link and report the same number for every country.\n\nLatency, not throughput. Each country used to get a twelve-second download that added nothing to the answer: meek moves data in HTTP round trips, so on this transport speed largely IS the round trip, and one request ranks the list. With that gone and the connect ceiling cut from 40s to 25s -- measured connects came in at 5-17s -- a six-country run went from about four minutes to two. Real sample: Germany 523ms, Britain 1144ms, Canada 1387ms, France 1774ms.\n\nAnd the country preference is finally applied. Two things stopped it: it was read only for chained runs, and the one attempt it did get dropped the protocol limit -- so on a line where only fronting works, a German preference spent its whole budget dialling German servers over protocols that cannot answer, timed out, and landed in the US every time. Dropping the limit was right when all five fronted servers were US and GB; with eleven across six countries, “fronted AND this country” is a real set, so the limit is kept whenever the chosen country has a fronted server to match it with.\n\nThree things it got wrong at first. It connected when it finished -- the “user switched it off” latch was left open and auto-reconnect reads exactly that state, so the app dialled a tunnel by itself the moment a measurement ended. A measurement must never decide to connect; it now sets the winning country as the preference and stops there. There was also no way to stop a run: the same row is the stop button now, and results so far are kept. And the country being measured says so on its own row rather than only in the header.\n\nPer-server measurement was attempted and is not in this build. `EgressRegion` names a country and lets Psiphon choose inside it; `TargetServerEntry` is the only way to name one server, and the core rejected every entry fed to it -- `newTargetServerEntryIterator` failed on all six candidates of the first country tried, so the pass reported “0 of 6 answered” about servers it had never actually reached. A number that is wrong is worse than no number.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Psiphon gets a fresh server list",
                            "`RemoteServerListURL` was set to an empty string, so the client had exactly the 430 server entries compiled into the APK and never learned another one. Five of those advertise domain fronting -- and on a line where the direct protocols are blocked, those five ARE the tunnel. Measured on the reporting device: the all-direct-protocols strategy timed out, the in-proxy strategy timed out, and only the fronted one connected, at 29 KB/s. Five candidates is not a pool, it is a bottleneck.\n\nPsiphon's public server list is now fetched, and a fresh copy of it is bundled: **493 servers instead of 430, and 11 fronted instead of 5.** Where they are matters more than how many -- all five of the old ones were US and GB, and the list now carries Germany, the Netherlands, France and Canada as well. meek is round-trip-bound, so a closer edge is directly a faster one. A second CDN (Fastly) joins Akamai. Measured on the same line and the same region: 29 KB/s to **41 KB/s**. The four endpoints and their ordering were read out of the official Psiphon client rather than invented, and the signing key this app already carried turns out to be byte-for-byte the one that signs that list -- which is what makes the fetch meaningful, since a wrongly-signed list is discarded by the core.",
                            Icons.Default.Public
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "An MTU field per method, and a button that measures it",
                            "Each of the nine now has its own MTU field: MASQUE, WireGuard, WARP-on-WARP, Psiphon, Tor, Quick Connect, MLM Gateway, SNI anti-filter and V2Ray. One number for all of them was the wrong shape, because they do not share a path. MASQUE wraps every inner packet in QUIC and loses 196 bytes to it, WireGuard loses 60, and Psiphon and Tor lose nothing at all -- tun2socks terminates the connection on the device, so their MTU is a local buffer size that never reaches the network.\n\nAnd a button that measures it: “find the best value for this line” sends don’t-fragment probes of exact sizes, halving the range each time, until it has the largest packet the line carries to the byte, then subtracts that method’s framing. The tunnel does not need to be connected, and that is not a shortcut: this app excludes its own package from its own tun, so a probe started from inside it rides the direct link whatever is running. So the direct link is what gets measured -- which is the same path the tunnel’s own outer packets take. Checked against hand measurements taken through each live tunnel from a shell, which is not excluded: MASQUE 1304, WireGuard 1440, WARP-on-WARP 1220, all three exact. WireGuard is the one that proves the arithmetic: 1500 − 1440 = 60, which is exactly 20 (IPv4) + 8 (UDP) + 32 (WireGuard) on paper.\n\nThe measurement found two bugs. WARP-on-WARP’s default was 1280 -- 60 bytes above what it can actually deliver -- so every full-size packet was dropped and the tunnel behaved exactly as reported: connects, then large pages hang. Correcting it to 1220 then stopped the tunnel coming up at all, with “Cannot set address”: 1280 is IPv6’s own minimum link MTU and the kernel refuses a v6 address below it. Dropping just the address was worse still -- `ip -6 route get` showed IPv6 leaving over wlan0 from the user’s real public address while the screen said connected, because Android discards a route whose family has no address, and a v6 DNS server was still being advertised. Below 1280 nothing of the v6 family is added now — no address, no route, no resolver — which is what makes VpnService block the family outright.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The MTU setting only reached one of the methods",
                            "The MTU field in Advanced settings was read by exactly one place in the app: the Xray service. The other five transports each carried their own hardcoded number and nothing could move any of them -- 1280 for MASQUE, WireGuard and WARP-in-WARP; 1500 for Psiphon, Tor, proxy mode and the chained path; and VPN Gate derived its own from a separate preference. Even on MASQUE inside the Xray service the value was only a ceiling: it could be lowered and never raised. A setting that silently applies to half the connections is worse than one that applies to none, because the user tunes it, sees no change, and has no way to learn which half they were in.\n\nAll seven places that write an MTU onto the interface now read it from one. Each transport's number became its DEFAULT rather than a cap: an untouched install behaves exactly as before, and a value the user sets is obeyed literally on every method -- including upward, which is the direction the old ceiling silently refused. The tun2socks paths hand lwIP the same number as the interface; it used to be a constant, and a mismatch there does not make a smaller packet, it makes a wrong one. In Settings the field reads \"automatic\" until something is stored, instead of showing 1280 -- a number that was true on none of the transports, since V2Ray ran at 1420 -- and what is typed is clamped once to 576-1500 on the way out and written back into the box, so the number on screen is the number that was kept. It used to save on every keystroke, so a half-typed \"1\" was stored as an MTU of 1.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A subscription link pointing at a JSON file",
                            "A link like that imported nothing. The subscription update read the body only as a list of links: it split it into lines and threw away every line that did not begin with `vless://` or a sibling — and a JSON document is dozens of lines, none of which is a config on its own, so all of it was discarded and the user was told \"no valid configs found\" about a subscription that was entirely valid configs. The whole document is now offered to the same JSON parser before the line splitting, and an array of several complete configs is accepted.\n\nTwo more obstacles sat in front of the same file. The picker did not show a JSON file at all: `ACTION_GET_CONTENT` opens a category gallery on several vendor builds — Samsung's among them — organised as images, video, audio and documents, and `.json` belongs to none of them, so a user who had downloaded the file could not find it however hard they looked. The system document browser opens now, which lists every file whatever its type. And selecting a file sometimes gave nothing but \"error adding file\": while downloading, Telegram writes the file under a temporary name, `name.json.cac`, and that name gets indexed by the media database; the finished file is then renamed and the row for the temporary one stays behind forever. It appears in the picker looking exactly like the real file and opening it fails with \"no such file\". That is not a bad config, and answering it with \"invalid config\" sends the user off to fix the one thing that was never wrong. Reading the file is its own step with its own message now, and it says the file could not be read and that a partial download may have been picked.",
                            Icons.Default.AttachFile
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "REALITY configs did not work at all",
                            "A REALITY link imported fine, sat in the list, and then measured no delay and refused to connect — while the same link worked in every other client. REALITY was simply never implemented: all three places where the app builds an Xray config wrote the security value through verbatim (\"reality\") and then always filled in the ordinary TLS block. The result announced REALITY and carried none of its inputs — no public key, no short id, no fingerprint, no spiderX — so Xray had nothing to perform the handshake with and the outbound never came up. All four values were parsed out of the link and then discarded. `flow` was dropped the same way: `xtls-rprx-vision` is not decoration, and a server configured for it rejects a user that arrives without it. The fingerprint is also no longer pinned to chrome — it is one of the things REALITY varies and the link carries it.",
                            Icons.Default.Https
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "An update badge beside the shield",
                            "Instead of a dialog opening over whatever the user was doing, a small icon now appears next to the shield at the top of the home screen when a newer release is found, and one tap goes straight to Settings › Software update. It is present only while there is something to go to: a control that is always visible and usually does nothing is one people stop seeing, and this one has to be noticed on the day it matters. Its tap target is larger than the glyph, because 16dp on its own is well under what a finger reliably hits.",
                            Icons.Default.SystemUpdateAlt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A 401 when saving a Sub Link to Cloudflare",
                            "Saving failed with \"API Error: 401 — Authentication error\" while the same account worked perfectly in the Cloudflare section. Which credential shape an account uses is decided once, when it is added, and trusted from then on — which is right, because the fallback shape test is narrower than reality: a working 52-character Global API Key has been seen on a real account and the test only recognises 37. But \"decided once, trusted forever\" has an obvious failure: if it was recorded wrongly, every later call authenticates the wrong way with no path back, and re-entering the same credential changes nothing. A 401 — the one answer that means the envelope is wrong — now triggers a single retry with the other header shape, and if that works the account is corrected on disk so the next call is right first time. A 403 or 404 is returned untouched: those are about permissions and missing resources, and retrying them with different headers would only hide them.",
                            Icons.Default.Key
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"Separate panels\" was forgotten when the app closed",
                            "The group-by-panel switch lived only as long as the screen was in memory, so closing and reopening the app turned it off. That switch changes the shape of the whole screen, which makes losing it read as \"my setting was never saved\" rather than as a default being restored. It is stored on disk now — along with the panel and folder that were open, because coming back to a grouped list that has forgotten which folder you were in is only half a restore. It is written in one place rather than at every point that changes it: there are several, and one of them forgetting is exactly this bug.",
                            Icons.Default.Layers
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Copy every working config, and see which folder you are acting on",
                            "List settings gained a \"copy the working configs\" action above the dead-config cleanup; \"working\" is the exact inverse of the test used for \"dead\", so both buttons speak from one definition. And that section is now headed with the folder or panel it applies to — \"Actions for 'EDG'\" instead of just \"Cleanup\". With separate panels on, the same screen deletes a different set depending on the tab behind it, and \"delete every config in this folder\" is not a button to press while guessing which folder that is.",
                            Icons.Default.ContentCopy
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The EDG panel is updated",
                            "The EDG worker moves from 29 July to 4 September — five upstream releases at once. The notable ones: ALPN is now carried automatically on node links, which suits more clients; a fix for HTTPS proxying, where the handshake failed when the upstream server asked for a client certificate; and several XHTTP improvements — small writes are coalesced before sending, the TCP path is piped directly instead of going through a write queue and chunk-by-chunk copying (noticeably less CPU), and a trailing slash in the path no longer breaks a reverse-proxy configuration. Before replacing it, all four values the app binds when it deploys the worker — UUID, PROXYIP, ADMIN and KV — were checked to be read under the same names in the new version, and the compatibility date was unchanged.",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Downloading an update on a line that blocks GitHub",
                            "The update screen showed the version and its size and then said \"Unable to resolve host github.com\". The check talks to api.github.com and the download to github.com, and the network does not treat those two the same: one resolved and the other was answered with nothing. So the app knew an update existed and could not fetch a byte of it. Two things changed. Names are no longer resolved by the network's own resolver — the thing doing the blocking — but over DNS-over-HTTPS to two resolvers addressed by IP; by IP, because a resolver named by hostname would need the very lookup that does not work. Both answers are returned together so a blocked address has another to fail over to: on the line this was reported from, one connected in 2.8 seconds and the other took 15. And when a tunnel is up, the download now goes through it. That could not happen by itself: the app is excluded from its own tunnel, so an ordinary request from inside it leaves on the carrier's link however healthy the tunnel is. Turning the VPN on now actually affects the download.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudDownload
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The automatic \"new version\" dialog is gone",
                            "A dialog used to appear over whatever the user was doing the moment a new version was found. Nobody asked for it at that moment — they were opening a tunnel, or reading a config — and an interruption that can only be dismissed teaches people to dismiss it without reading. Checking and installing now live in Settings › Software update, on the user's own initiative. The check itself is kept: it is silent, it costs one request, and it is what makes that page answer instantly instead of starting from \"checking…\" every time it is opened.",
                            Icons.Default.NotificationsOff
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The V2Ray crash — 74 users, one cause",
                            "Found by reports users sent themselves. The ping, delay and speed tests wrote their result by POSITION in the list, using an index taken from a snapshot made before any measurement began. A test runs for minutes and the list moves underneath it — a free-config import, a subscription refresh, a deleted node. Two outcomes: if the list had shifted, a result landed on the wrong row and two rows ended up carrying one id, which the list throws on when scrolled (65 users); if it had shrunk, the index ran off the end (9 users, one of them \"Index 809 out of bounds for length 8\"). Every write is now keyed by id, which does not care about order and cannot produce a duplicate.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "And a repair for lists that were already corrupted",
                            "Because the bad write was followed by a save, the damage was on disk: those users crashed again on every launch even with correct code. The list is now deduplicated as it is read, and the next save writes the repaired version back.",
                            Icons.Default.HealthAndSafety
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"You have no subdomain\" was reported as an unknown cause",
                            "Seven users sent a report, and all seven were the same thing: Cloudflare answering \"You do not have a workers.dev subdomain\" with code 10007 and status 404. The troubleshooter only recognised the 200 form of that answer, so no branch matched and it fell through to \"the cause is not something this version recognises — send the report\". The people the screen exists for were asked for a report instead of being offered the button that fixes it, and the answer was inside the code they were sending.",
                            Icons.Default.Language
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A large wallpaper, two different crashes on two devices",
                            "A custom photo was only downsampled when the requested size was known, and on the first frame it is still zero. So an eight-thousand-pixel image was decoded whole and cached: on one phone the canvas refused to draw it (\"262440000 bytes\") and on another that one bitmap took nearly the whole 256MB heap, and the app ran out of memory on a 16-byte allocation in Settings. The size ceiling no longer depends on the request.",
                            Icons.Default.Image
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "On Android 7 the first connect closed the app",
                            "Notification channels are Android 8 and later; this app supports Android 7, and the tunnel service's notification path called them with no version check. The try/catch around it did not help, because NoSuchMethodError is an Error rather than an Exception and passes straight through.",
                            Icons.Default.NotificationsOff
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A crash when opening WireGuard just after the service stopped",
                            "Android can deliver a start request to a service that is already being torn down. The work was handed to an executor that had been shut down, and the rejection closed the app. It is now dropped and logged — the service that would have run it no longer exists.",
                            Icons.Default.PowerSettingsNew
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "An Android 8 platform bug that closed the app",
                            "The per-node country lookup used HttpURLConnection, which is backed by Android's own internal copy of OkHttp, and on Android 8.0 that copy has a race in its connection pool: the cleanup thread reads the address of a socket that has already been released and throws. It lands on a thread the app does not own, with not one frame of ours in the stack, so nothing here could catch it. The app already ships its own OkHttp, which does not have the bug; this was the only place still using the platform's.",
                            Icons.Default.Android
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.31",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The home screen on a tablet, a television, DeX and a phone turned sideways",
                            "The icon grid was a fixed 4x6 — right for a phone held upright and wrong for everything else: four columns ran down the middle with two thirds of the width empty, and the rows were squeezed into a strip too short to hold them. The shape is now derived from the space it actually has, up to six columns. An upright phone gets exactly the 4x6 it always had, deliberately and by an early return, because that is what every existing user's saved icon order was arranged against. Rows are bounded by two ceilings rather than one — what fits AND what is needed. With only the first, a tablet computed six rows for nineteen icons, packed them into the top four, and left the bottom third of the screen empty.",
                            Icons.Default.GridView
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Paged icons",
                            "Icons that do not fit on one page go onto the next, with page dots beneath them — so nothing is ever hidden below the bottom edge, and no dot appears when there is no overflow. A single dot under a board that cannot be swiped is an invitation to try something that does nothing.",
                            Icons.Default.MoreHoriz
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "In landscape the icons sat off-centre on the screen",
                            "The display cutout is on one edge and the navigation bar on the other, but the home screen padded only top and bottom. The icons were centred in the window and visibly off-centre on the screen. Horizontal insets are now taken from both. The dock no longer fills the width either: unchanged on a phone, and a centred bar on anything larger instead of four icons at the far corners of a two-foot rail.",
                            Icons.Default.AlignHorizontalCenter
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Android TV: it now works with a remote",
                            "The app declared LEANBACK, so it installed on Android TV and appeared in the launcher — and then nothing worked. Every control was built on pointerInput, which answers a finger and knows nothing about a directional pad; there was no focus anywhere in the tree. A television showed a board of icons that could be looked at and never opened, which is worse than not shipping on TV at all because it looks like a finished app. Three things were added, and leaving out any one produces a failure that looks like a different bug: focusability (or the pad cannot reach it), a key handler (or it can be reached and pressing does nothing), and a visible ring (or focus moves invisibly and the remote appears dead). No separate TV layout was built — the tablet board is already the right shape on a television.",
                            Icons.Default.Tv
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Settings is two panes on a large screen",
                            "The list on one side, the open page on the other. In Persian the list lands on the right and in English on the left — and that comes from the layout itself rather than from a branch on the language, because Compose mirrors a Row under right-to-left. A language branch would be a second place to keep in step and would be wrong for every RTL language not yet thought about. The same route switch is rendered twice, once for the list and once for the detail, so the two panes cannot drift apart.",
                            Icons.Default.ViewSidebar
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The sidebar no longer vanishes when a sub-page opens",
                            "\"Advanced VPN settings\" and \"Usage\" left Settings for a tab of their own. Invisible on a phone; in two panes it meant the sidebar disappeared the moment either was opened. They now open in the detail pane. The same was fixed on the transport screens: advanced settings, the connection report, the country pickers and Tor's mode all open in the second column, and the connect control — the one thing the screen exists for — stays where it was.",
                            Icons.Default.Dock
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "MASQUE, WireGuard, WARP-on-WARP, Psiphon and Tor are two columns",
                            "One column for what you look at (the connect control, the status, the live counters) and one for what you act on (the options and the pages behind them). All five transports render from one shared screen, so all five changed together. The Cloud screen follows the same shape: the account list on one side, the open account or group on the other.",
                            Icons.Default.ViewColumn
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The wallpaper preview grew enormous",
                            "The preview scaled with its container, and in a tablet's detail pane that produced a single image roughly a foot tall that pushed every control under it off the screen. It is a picture of a phone screen, so its useful size is fixed, and it is now capped. The preset gallery had the same fault: two equal-weight columns each taking nearly five hundred dp.",
                            Icons.Default.Image
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "LAN sharing: the app died on the very first device that connected",
                            "A second device would connect, the phone would show it as connected, and then not one byte moved. The cause was an uncaught exception in the local relay: the reverse direction ran in a pooled Runnable that had try/finally and no catch, and closing the socket at the end of every ordinary connection threw there — the exception escaped the Runnable, reached that thread's default handler, and on Android that ends the process. So the app died on the first connection, which is also why no traffic ever moved. Both directions now catch, the close order was changed so a response still arriving is not cut off, and relay threads carry their own handler: a single missed catch can no longer take the app down.",
                            Icons.Default.Lan
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Half-open connections, and a share that dropped mid-download",
                            "Two faults that only appear under real load. First: when one side half-closed and then said nothing, the socket sat in FIN_WAIT2 holding two threads for as long as the app lived — on the test phone, 12 of the 18 sockets on the relay port were stuck exactly like that. Now when one direction ends, the other gets 90 seconds and then both sockets are closed. Second: the relay was brought up and down from a two-second poll, so a single flickering reading — a VPN service reconnecting, say — was enough to drop every LAN client mid-download. Three consecutive polls must now agree before it comes down.",
                            Icons.Default.Timer
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Proxy Mode was never read for MASQUE, WireGuard or WARP-on-WARP",
                            "The switch only ever affected the Xray family. The service that carries those three transports never read it — its own comment said \"VPN mode is the only mode, there is no proxy branch any more\". So a user turned it on, nothing happened, and the Local Network screen told them to turn on that same switch to share — advice that was impossible to follow for exactly the engines it was given for. Turning it on now genuinely works: the core comes up behind tun2socks and publishes a local listener. This is not \"proxy instead of VPN\" — the whole phone stays tunnelled; there is simply now something another device can connect to. With the switch off, the connect path is exactly what it was and not one line of this code runs.",
                            Icons.Default.SettingsEthernet
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "In Proxy Mode nothing would open, on a tunnel that was perfectly healthy",
                            "tun2socks needs a udpgw server for UDP and reaches it from inside the tunnel, and because DNS is transparent, every DNS query goes down that same path. Psiphon has such a server; the Rust core does not. Measured on the phone: a request to example.com through the tunnel fetched the page, while the identical request to the udpgw address hung forever. So TCP was carried flawlessly and no name resolved — which reads to a user as \"connected, and nothing opens\". The fix was what Tor already had: a local SOCKS front that implements the udpgw protocol. For the Rust core, DNS now goes over TCP through the tunnel to your own backend resolver — no query leaves outside it.",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The relay now speaks HTTP itself",
                            "The Rust core, Psiphon and Tor understand only SOCKS5, but the one setting Windows, macOS, iOS and Android all accept is a PAC address, and the only entry all of them act on is an HTTP proxy — Android has no usable SOCKS support in its Wi-Fi settings at all. So those engines were unshareable in practice. The relay now reads the first byte: a SOCKS client is passed through untouched, anything else is answered as HTTP and translated into a SOCKS5 CONNECT. Hostnames are deliberately not resolved locally, because that is a DNS leak over the phone's own link. A side effect: Psiphon and Tor became shareable too, and the two-port HTTP/SOCKS split is gone from the PAC.",
                            Icons.Default.SwapHoriz
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Send a crash report with one button",
                            "The app already saved every crash on the phone with the full stack and the trail of screens leading to it, but there was no way for that file to reach us — and for a bug several users report and that never reproduces on our own phones, that is the only thing actually needed. The next run after a crash now asks, and one button sends it. The report carries the stack, the phone model and the app version, and never your configs or your traffic. A bug in the recorder itself was found and fixed along the way: the filename had one-second resolution, so several threads dying at once all wrote to one file and the result was zero bytes — the report was destroyed by exactly the kind of crash it existed to explain.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "SNI configs no longer appear in the V2Ray section",
                            "Building an SNI config copied the source config and overrode only its address and folder, so the engine type was inherited too — and the V2Ray screen builds its tabs from that type. The result was SNI configs sitting inside the BPB folder, where they cannot work unless the SNI engine happens to be up. They are now filtered out of the V2Ray list and appear only on their own screen. The filter is deliberately applied to the display rather than to the list itself: several actions on that screen write the whole list back to disk, so filtering the source would delete every SNI config the first time anyone measured a ping.",
                            Icons.Default.FilterAlt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Turning SNI on lit two lamps",
                            "Both the SNI lamp and the V2Ray lamp, for one connection. The test for \"this is an SNI session\" was a lookup of the connected node in the config list — and that list loads from disk asynchronously, so during the home screen's first composition after launch it was still empty, the answer came back \"not SNI\", and because nothing in that computation's cache key changed, it stayed wrong. The service carrying the config now publishes the fact itself — the same place that already worked it out in order to bring the SNI front up.",
                            Icons.Default.VpnLock
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The wrong \"fill in your email\" advice for someone who pasted a token",
                            "In the Cloudflare account troubleshooter, when adding an account failed, the first and most prominent advice was that a Global API Key does not work without an email — and its only test was \"the email box is blank\". But an API token needs no email at all. So anyone who correctly pasted a token and had it rejected was sent after a problem they did not have, in front of the one they did. The screen already displayed which header the key was sent with two rows higher; it simply was not consulted here. That advice now appears only when a Global API Key really was sent.",
                            Icons.Default.AlternateEmail
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The eight free configs can no longer be shared",
                            "The share section is removed for those eight. The QR card went with it, not just the copy and send buttons: the QR encodes the same link, and removing the buttons while leaving it would close the two obvious routes and leave the one that needs no tap at all — a photograph of the screen.",
                            Icons.Default.Lock
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Importing a config in JSON format",
                            "\"From the clipboard\" and \"from a file\" were both line-based and accepted only links, and a JSON config is a single document hundreds of lines long, no line of which is a config on its own — so it always came back \"invalid config\". The app could already connect one (the eight Iran configs are exactly this); there was simply no way to get one in. The whole text is now read as one JSON document first; both a single object and an array of several are accepted, and the name is taken from the remarks field.",
                            Icons.Default.DataObject
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The \"email not verified\" block that was never true",
                            "Several people with a fully verified Cloudflare account were held behind \"Verify Email (Required)\" with no way out, because the \"check status\" button re-ran the same broken check. The cause: the app read an `email_verified` field from Cloudflare's user endpoint, and that endpoint has no such field — so the answer was \"not verified\" for every account that did not already have a workers.dev subdomain, and for token credentials the endpoint could not even be read. That inference is gone. Only Cloudflare can now say an email is unverified, and only by refusing to create the subdomain for that reason. A block already written to an account is cleared once, automatically.",
                            Icons.Default.LockOpen
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A Cloudflare account troubleshooting page",
                            "A wrench beside each account, and an entrance on the add screen when adding fails. Three parts: **what was seen** — what Cloudflare answered just now, the header shape, the status code and Cloudflare's own error code; **the suggestion**, derived from that error code, with exactly one button next to it; and **change it by hand**, for when the suggestion does not fit your account. None of it changes how the app behaves for anyone else: every change lands on that account alone and can be changed back.",
                            Icons.Default.Build
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "When the account will not add at all",
                            "\"The credentials were rejected\" was one sentence for four completely different situations: a Global API Key pasted without its account email, a revoked or expired token, a token that authenticates but may not see the account, and a phone that never reached Cloudflare. A \"Why did this fail?\" button now sits under that error and separates the four — with nothing saved yet.",
                            Icons.Default.PersonAdd
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Subdomain creation fixed for token accounts",
                            "That call sent the Global-API-Key headers unconditionally, with no token branch — the same bug found and fixed in the status probe, and missed here. For a token account it put the token in the Global-Key header, which Cloudflare does not reject cleanly: it authenticates the account and then refuses the resource with error 10037. So \"create subdomain\" failed for every token account no matter what permissions it had.",
                            Icons.Default.Language
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Diagnostic reports, from where you are stuck",
                            "The pipe that carries crash reports now carries diagnostics too, grouped in the dashboard by *failure class* rather than by count — so \"how many users, and which cause\" is answerable at a glance. A report carries the status codes, the app version and the phone model only; your token, email address and workers.dev name are never in it.",
                            Icons.Default.Send
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The new-version notice is a notice",
                            "The \"a new version is out\" dialog used to download the APK itself, through a screen that existed nowhere else in the app. That gave the updater two front doors with different behaviour: this one knew nothing about the auto-download-over-Wi-Fi setting, could not tell the file was already on disk, and left the user on a full-screen downloader they had not navigated to and could not find again. It now says what it knows and hands over to Settings › Software update › Download and install — one place to install from, and one you can return to.",
                            Icons.Default.SystemUpdate
                        ),
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.3",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Proxy password, per-device blocking, and a share timer",
                            "Three optional protections, all off by default. **Proxy password** is a generated six-digit code that covers both SOCKS5 and HTTP from one setting and goes into the setup link automatically, so nothing still has to be typed. **Blocking** a device is a button on its own row; its traffic is then discarded. **The share timer** can be 30 minutes, an hour or two, after which sharing switches itself off — stored as a deadline rather than a countdown, so closing the app does not quietly extend it. And a fourth switch, \"only on my own hotspot\", puts the proxy back on this phone alone the moment it joins someone else's Wi-Fi.",
                            Icons.Default.Key
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Tor and GST can be shared now too",
                            "Both published their listener on this phone only, so the Local Network screen would report a healthy share that no device could actually connect to. Both now listen on every interface while sharing is on, and behave exactly as before when it is off. Tor's proxy takes no password, and the screen says so rather than offering a switch that does nothing.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The address in the notification shade, and a Quick Settings tile",
                            "While sharing is on, the address stays in a notification with two buttons: copy address, and stop sharing. The reason is simple — you are usually holding the phone in one hand and typing on the other device with the other, and coming back into the app to re-read an IP address is exactly what this feature exists to remove. A Quick Settings tile was added alongside, with the address as its subtitle, or the reason there isn't one.",
                            Icons.Default.Notifications
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A route diagram, and the six guides on the phone itself",
                            "Four boxes at the top of the screen — other device, this phone, tunnel, internet — each lit independently. Deliberately not a progress bar: the tunnel can drop while a client is still attached, so the third box goes grey while the first stays green, and a bar filling left to right cannot say that. Lower down are step-by-step guides for Windows, macOS, iOS, Android, Linux and TV/console — the same text the other device sees, from one source, for a device with no browser or for when you are reading them out to someone else.",
                            Icons.Default.Route
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Local Network — give another device this phone's connection",
                            "A new icon on the home screen. This feature used to be a single switch in Settings called \"Allow LAN Connection\", and that switch was its entire interface: no address to enter on the other device, no way to tell whether anyone had connected, and — worst — no sign that the engine currently running might publish no proxy at all, in which case the switch was on and nothing whatsoever happened. It is now a full screen that states what is actually true and puts the control that fixes each problem right underneath it.",
                            Icons.Default.Lan
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Scan one QR code — nobody types a number",
                            "Setting a proxy by hand means moving three values — address, port and kind — from a phone screen to another device, through a settings dialog most people have never opened. So the phone serves the instructions instead. The other device scans a code or opens a link and gets a page that has worked out from its own browser whether it is Windows, macOS, iPhone or Android, and shows only those steps — with the option names written exactly as they appear on that device's screen. Windows also gets a one-line PowerShell command that does the whole thing at once.",
                            Icons.Default.QrCode2
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A PAC file — one address instead of three fields",
                            "Windows, macOS, iOS and Android all have a field for an automatic proxy configuration URL, and the phone now generates that file itself. Because its contents are ours, it excludes local network addresses from the tunnel — your printer, router and NAS do not get pointlessly routed through the phone — and offers both HTTP and SOCKS5 on the same port. One deliberate decision: it never returns DIRECT. A PAC that falls back to a direct connection when the tunnel drops sends the other device's traffic out in the clear at exactly the moment protection mattered.",
                            Icons.Default.SettingsEthernet
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A live answer to \"did it work?\", from both ends",
                            "The permanent problem with any proxy instructions is that the user follows them and cannot tell whether it took. The phone now says \"waiting for the first device…\" and then shows the address of whichever one connects; and on the other device, the setup page asks the phone every two seconds and reports one of three states: not set up yet, set up but no connection seen, or connected. No external service is involved — both answers come from the kernel's own connection table.",
                            Icons.Default.CheckCircle
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Ten-step diagnostics, each row with the button that fixes it",
                            "\"It doesn't work\" has six different causes here and they all look identical from outside. The diagnostics check ten things separately. The most valuable row is whether any device has opened the setup page, because it splits the problem in half: if one has but has not connected, the fault is in that device's proxy settings; if none has, the two machines cannot see each other at all — which usually means the Wi-Fi keeps its clients apart, as cafe, hotel and office networks routinely do, and the fix is the phone's own hotspot. The report copies out as text.",
                            Icons.Default.MedicalServices
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A hotspot from inside the app, with a Wi-Fi QR code",
                            "If the phone is on no network at all, the hotspot button is right there. Android generates the name and password, and we put a Wi-Fi QR code next to them that Android and iPhone cameras read directly — so nobody has to read a generated password aloud. The location permission Android requires for a hotspot is asked for only at the moment the button is tapped, with the reason stated there. If the phone refuses, a second button opens Android's own tethering settings.",
                            Icons.Default.WifiTethering
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Engines that cannot share stop pretending they can",
                            "MASQUE, WireGuard and WARP-on-WARP take the whole device in VPN mode and publish no local proxy; AmneziaWG and VPN Gate are the same. Until now the switch stayed on and nothing happened. The screen now states plainly what the running engine can and cannot do, and for the first three offers both routes side by side: turn on proxy mode, which brings the same engine up with a proxy, or choose a different engine. Neither happens automatically — an automatic switch means a sudden tunnel drop in the middle of your work.",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A list of connected devices, with no permission at all",
                            "The usual way to do this is the ARP table, which has returned an empty result to ordinary apps since Android 10. We read the app's own TCP connection table instead — the same restriction that broke ARP makes this precise, because every row it returns is by definition a connection to our own proxy. Device names are deliberately not guessed at; only the address is shown.",
                            Icons.Default.Devices
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A warning that looks at the network under your feet",
                            "The shared proxy has no password, but that means one thing on your own hotspot and something else entirely on a cafe's Wi-Fi. A single paragraph of text used to say both and therefore neither. The app now works out from the network interface name whether you are on your own hotspot or somebody else's network, and warns only in the second case. The default behaviour is deliberately unchanged — changing it would mean someone whose laptop connects over home Wi-Fi today is cut off tomorrow after an update, with no idea why.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The settings entry was renamed",
                            "\"Allow LAN Connection\" became \"Local Network Connection\", and its page is now titled \"Local Network\". Every new string in this feature — the screen, the diagnostics, and even the web page served to the other device — exists in both languages. Only the Windows, macOS and iOS option names are left untranslated, because you are looking for those exact words on screen. Numbers stay in Latin digits either way: an IP written in Persian digits cannot be typed into anything.",
                            Icons.Default.Translate
                        ),
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.2",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The home screen is rebuilt — every feature is an icon",
                            "The hamburger drawer is gone entirely. The twelve features that were hidden behind it — Fixed IP, Workers, anti-sanction DNS, Sub Link, Usage, Tutorials, About and all three emergency modes — now have icons on the home screen, next to Quick Connect, Cloud and Game Boost. The layout is four columns by six rows, and the four you reach for most — Scanner, V2Ray, Settings and WireGuard — stay in a glass dock at the bottom. The reasoning is simple: a feature inside a slide-out menu does not really exist until someone opens that menu.",
                            Icons.Default.Apps
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The cards are frosted glass now, the way iOS does it",
                            "The grouped setting cards and the home screen dock are no longer a flat translucent fill: each one shows a blurred slice of the wallpaper that is genuinely behind it. Compose has no backdrop blur and `Modifier.blur` does not exist below Android 12, so this is built the way a compositor would build it \u2014 one small blurred copy of the whole backdrop, work out which rectangle of it a card is covering, and draw that slice stretched back to the card\'s size. Because the copy is an eighth of the resolution the upscale IS the blur, and one shared bitmap serves every card on screen rather than each allocating its own. The corners were rounded to match iOS as well.",
                            Icons.Default.AutoAwesome
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Light and dark, icon size, and where the wallpaper reaches",
                            "Three new settings under Display. **Icon Size** scales the icon tile only \u2014 captions and cell spacing stay put, because scaling the whole cell would merely re-space the board and leave the icons looking the same size \u2014 with a live sample above the slider. **Wallpaper on every screen** can be turned off to give every screen except the home screen a plain background. **Appearance** switches between light, dark and automatic \u2014 with one honest limit: only the rebuilt surfaces (the home screen, Settings and everything under it, Advanced VPN, Total Usage) resolve their colours through the theme. The remaining screens still carry hardcoded dark values and are being converted one at a time, which is why the setting defaults to Dark rather than Automatic.",
                            Icons.Default.Contrast
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Settings is rebuilt too",
                            "Laid out exactly the way iOS lays out Settings: inset grouped cards, every row led by a small tinted glyph, the value in grey on the trailing side, and separators that start after the glyph rather than at the card edge. The settings are sorted into five groups — Network, Display, Usage, System and About. At the top, where iOS shows your Apple Account, sits your connected Cloudflare account: account name, email, and a cloud glyph on Cloudflare\'s own orange in place of a profile photo; tapping it takes you to the Cloud tab. One more change that matters: **the Save button is gone and every change applies immediately**, as it does on iOS. That is not only cosmetic — with a Save button, a screen whose changes had been saved and one whose changes had not looked identical. The single exception is the local port, which writes only while the number validates and tells you why when it does not, because a half-typed number is both a valid integer and a terrible port. Nothing opens a dropdown in place any more either: language, screen-off timeout, backend DNS and local port each push a page of their own, and each row shows its current value in grey beside it. Advanced VPN and Total Usage were rebuilt the same way. A new entry was added while we were here: \"Reset Home Screen Layout\".",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Wallpaper — ten ready-made looks, or your own photo",
                            "A new Settings section, Display > Wallpaper, carries ten backdrops — Dusk, Amber, Indigo, Charcoal, Morning Mist, Violet, Ocean, Slate, Sunset and Forest — or you can pick a photo from your gallery. The picker is Android's own, so the app never asks for gallery access. The ten presets are drawn by the app rather than shipped as image files, so they add nothing to the download and stay sharp on any screen size.",
                            Icons.Default.Palette
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Arrange the icons yourself",
                            "Hold your finger on any icon until they start to wobble, then drag and drop it wherever you like. The order is saved the moment you let go and survives closing the app. Leave the mode with the \"Done\" button at the top or the phone's back button. And if a later update adds a feature, its icon joins the end of YOUR layout rather than resetting it.",
                            Icons.Default.TouchApp
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Genuinely full screen — no cut line at the top",
                            "The wallpaper now runs under the clock, signal and battery icons and under the navigation bar at the bottom, so the black strip across the top is gone. The same change fixed an older fault: several full-screen pages (the emergency screens, Tutorials, About) were applying the system bar insets twice and sat too far down the screen.",
                            Icons.Default.PhoneAndroid
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Four new categories for free configs",
                            "The Windows app's four pools are now on Android too: Verified, Secure, Fast and All. You choose the source before the count, and each one shows how many configs it holds and what makes it different from the others. The sources refresh every 15 minutes.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudDownload
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Configs that were never getting tested",
                            "Three separate causes, all fixed. One: the Xray core was being re-initialised for every config, which under 12-16 concurrent tests meant some never started at all; it now initialises once for the life of the app. Two: the test timeouts were shorter than the app's own, so slow-but-working servers were being recorded as dead. Three: testing your saved list and searching the server pool shared one stop flag, so the end of any search aborted a running list test mid-way and left rows stuck on \"testing\" forever.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The server list opens offline",
                            "Opening the server list no longer waits on a download — the list you already have appears immediately, with a note at the top saying how old it is. When you want a fresh one you press \"Update servers\", and from the moment you press it you see each source being fetched and how far along it is.",
                            Icons.Default.Storage
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Targeted search takes any number you like",
                            "It used to offer a few fixed choices. Now you type the number: 13 countries, 9 countries, 15 servers, 120 servers — whatever you want, with plus/minus buttons or straight from the keyboard.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "OpenVPN core removed — the 16 KB compatibility warning is gone",
                            "Newer phones such as the Galaxy S26 showed a compatibility warning the moment the app opened. The cause was one native library that did not line up with Android's new 16 KB memory pages. It belonged to the OpenVPN core, which in practice never ran: MLM Gateway uses the SoftEther protocol, and the OpenVPN path was unreachable. It is gone, and all nine remaining libraries are 16 KB compatible. MLM Gateway itself is untouched.",
                            Icons.Default.Compress
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"Quick Connect\" — the app's landing screen, with a country picker",
                            "Quick Connect is the first icon on the app's home screen. It carries a ready-made list of thousands of public servers that downloads itself: no account, no panel to deploy, no config to paste. You pick a country from a flag list (each row showing how many servers it has, with a search box), press \"Find a server\", and the app hunts through them for ones that actually work. The list is kept on the phone for half an hour, and if a later download fails the previous list is shown instead — yesterday's servers can get you online, a failed download cannot.",
                            Icons.Default.FlashOn
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Every server gets a flag without being tested first",
                            "A server's country is read out of its own name: a flag character inside the name, patterns like \"DE1\" and \"NL12\", and over 60 countries along with their city names (Frankfurt, Amsterdam, Istanbul and so on). This happens on the phone with no network request at all, so flags appear immediately. An IP database is deliberately not used: across a 2827-server feed it agreed with the server's real country only 34% of the time — Cloudflare addresses and hosts like OVH are registered in one country and answer from another. Country names are shown in Persian.",
                            Icons.Default.Flag
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A server's real country is measured after connecting, and corrected",
                            "The flag a feed gives a server is only a claim. Once you are actually connected, the app makes a request to Cloudflare through that same tunnel and asks where the traffic came out. If the answer differs from the claim, the measurement wins: the server moves under its real country for good, shows up when you pick that country from then on, and disappears from the one it was falsely claiming — and you are told at that moment that the move happened. The reason is simple: someone who picks a country wants to come out in it. One exception is respected: when the path runs over WARP, Cloudflare reports YOUR country by design rather than the exit's, so that case is shown only as \"route verified\" and no country is recorded. Readings are kept for a month (server addresses get reassigned) and can be cleared any time from the country picker itself.",
                            Icons.Default.Verified
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A connect button, like the Windows app",
                            "Instead of picking a server first and connecting second, there is now one big button in the middle of the screen that always means the same thing: press it and you end up online. If you have saved servers it uses the fastest; if you have none it goes looking, finds three working ones, connects to the best and saves the other two so the next press needs no search at all. Its four states each get their own colour, motion and label: ready (breathing), searching/connecting (a rotating blue arc), connected (a steady green ring — motion on a settled state is just noise), and disconnecting (a red arc sweeping the other way).",
                            Icons.Default.PowerSettingsNew
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"My servers\" under the connect button",
                            "Servers you have tested and that worked stay under the connect button, and are still there after the app is closed. Each row has its own re-test and delete buttons, and the list header carries \"test all\" and \"delete\". Deleting asks whether you mean \"only the dead ones\" or \"all\". New entries are marked \"new\" and dead ones \"down\".",
                            Icons.Default.Bookmarks
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A \"Server list\" screen with \"Check every server\"",
                            "Browsing countries is now separate from the connect screen. There you pick a country and either press \"Quick check\" (up to 20 working servers, for when you just want to be online) or \"Check every server\", which tests every single server in that country. Once tested, one button moves all the results onto the connect screen. Each row says whether it was \"already tested\" or is \"new\", and the header states that the sources refresh every 15 minutes.",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A server you delete does not come back",
                            "Deleting a server used to only clear it from the screen, and the next list refresh brought it straight back. Deleted servers are now kept in a list of their own on the phone and dropped while the sources are being read, before they can enter the list at all — so they are not shown, not tested, and not re-downloaded. They are identified by the server's real address rather than its name, so a source republishing the same server under a different name does not resurrect it. If you change your mind, there is a \"restore\" button at the bottom of the server list screen.",
                            Icons.Default.DeleteSweep
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "VMess and Shadowsocks support — 21x more servers",
                            "The app read only VLESS and Trojan, so every VMess and Shadowsocks config in the sources was silently thrown away. Both are now supported (VMess with its own base64 blob, Shadowsocks in both of its common spellings — legacy and SIP002). Shadowsocks configs that need a side plugin are deliberately refused, because the core will start them and then carry no traffic. Alongside this, four more large sources were added (an aggregator that merges ~21 public sources every 15 minutes and files them by its own test results: verified, fast, secure, all). Between the two changes, the usable server count went from about 420 to about 9000.",
                            Icons.Default.Storage
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Finding a working server — two tests, but at the same time",
                            "The search runs two tests: a cheap check of whether the server's port is open at all, then a real connection. The second is necessary because on a filtered line most hosts accept the connection and then carry nothing — only that test proves anything. What changed is that the two no longer run one after the other. The first version swept the whole pool for reachability and only then began real-testing; with a pool now near 9000 servers that meant minutes of nothing happening, usually ending in no connection at all. Servers that pass the cheap check now flow straight into the real test in parallel, so the first genuine result is ready within seconds of pressing the button while the sweep continues behind it.",
                            Icons.Default.Search
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Quick Connect is now actually quick",
                            "The button connects to the first server it finds, **unless that server is slow** — the first host to answer is not necessarily one worth using, and the difference between 300ms and 1500ms is the difference between a page loading and a page crawling. So a first result under the threshold connects immediately, and anything worse buys a few more seconds of looking before it settles for the best it found. More importantly: if you already have saved servers it does not touch the pool at all — it tests just your own list in parallel and connects to the fastest. They are re-tested rather than trusted, because a saved number can be hours old and the server may have died since.",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Targeted search: \"50 servers from 5 countries\"",
                            "On the server list screen you pick a server count (10/25/50/100) and a country count (1/3/5/10) and get exactly that — every result proven by a real connection, spread evenly across the countries. \"50 from 5 countries\" has to mean 10 from each, or it is just \"50 servers\" with a filter on top and one popular country supplies all of them. The countries are swept concurrently rather than one after another, and a country that cannot fill its share is topped up from the others so a request for 50 comes back with 50.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"MLM Gateway\" got its own icon",
                            "The MLM Gateway (VPN Gate) is no longer the centre button on the bottom bar; it has its own icon on the home screen. It is otherwise unchanged and every one of its features is where it was.",
                            Icons.Default.Public
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed \"all sources failed\" in the server list",
                            "Sometimes every source in a row reported \"failed\" with an unreadable English message as the reason. The download of the list was tied to the screen itself, so leaving the screen cancelled it half-way \u2014 and worse, that cancellation was mistakenly recorded as \"this source's download error\", repeated for every source in turn. The download now runs independently of the screen, and a cancellation is no longer mistaken for a real failure.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudOff
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the wrong country right after connecting",
                            "A server would sometimes be identified as \"Iran\" at first and then show its real country (Sweden, say) after a disconnect and reconnect. The country check ran on a fixed delay and never verified that the request had actually gone through the tunnel; if the core was not ready yet, the answer was your own country, and that was recorded as the server's. The app now keeps your own internet address as a baseline while disconnected, and if the answer matches it, it knows the traffic did not go through the tunnel: it is not recorded, no flag is set, and it says \"connected, but the exit country was not verified\" instead. It also retries until the answer genuinely comes from the tunnel.",
                            Icons.Default.GpsOff
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the proxy warning when switching servers",
                            "Tapping another server while connected started the new one without closing the old one first. Two cores then existed at once and the second could not take the local port because the first still held it \u2014 the English warning you saw. Worse, the status and country check would then be talking to the previous tunnel, which was one of the causes of the wrong country. Connecting now fully closes the previous connection and waits for the port to be released.",
                            Icons.Default.SwapHoriz
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Connection tab: a \"down only\" option next to \"delete all\"",
                            "The delete button did one thing: wipe every config in that tab or folder. It now offers two, with the safer one first — \"Down only\", with the count shown on the button itself. **Configs that have never been tested are never deleted** — only ones actually measured whose result was Timeout, Error or N/A. A config whose test you cancelled part-way is also kept, because cancelling says nothing about whether the server works. A config that answers a ping but fails the real connection test does count as down, since a ping only proves something is listening, not that traffic gets through. Protected Iran configs are left alone as before.",
                            Icons.Default.DeleteSweep
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Leaving the server list no longer cancels the search",
                            "Pressing back mid-check used to destroy the whole search and lose every server found so far — on \"check every server\" that is minutes of work, lost to looking at another screen. The search now runs independently of the screen: back simply leaves, the search carries on in the background, and returning finds the same run with its results intact. Stopping is done only with the \"stop\" button.",
                            Icons.Default.ArrowBack
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the Local Port setting — it used to accept any number at all",
                            "The field did no checking of what you typed, and a bad value never announced itself. Besides the port you enter, the app also uses \"port + 10000\". So 21000 put that second port on 31000 — the range the app uses for testing servers — and anything above 55535 meant the second port could not exist at all. The result was \"every server tests as dead\" or \"the status bar never finds the country\", neither of which looks like a port problem. Now an unsuitable number is explained right there and Save stays disabled until it is fixed. Every part of the app follows the same single rule, and server testing can no longer land on the port a live tunnel is holding.",
                            Icons.Default.SettingsEthernet
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the back button on the landing screen",
                            "On the landing screen the phone's back button moved sideways into the Cloud tab instead of offering to exit, which meant the app could not be left with back from the one screen that is supposed to own that. Fixed.",
                            Icons.Default.ArrowBack
                        ),
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.1",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "New \"Domain Fronting\" folder — no server at all, browser only",
                            "A new folder in the connection tab carrying a config that opens YouTube, Instagram, WhatsApp, Facebook and Reddit \"in a browser only\" (Chrome or anything similar). The YouTube and Instagram apps do not work with this method — that is an Android restriction with no way around it, so if you need the apps themselves this option will not do it for you. What it does give you is that it needs no server and no worker anywhere in the path. Nothing to deploy, nothing to pay for, and no shared bandwidth to saturate no matter how many people use it, because the traffic goes straight out from your own phone. The whole setup happens inside that folder: the app mints a certificate unique to your device, then one button saves it to your Downloads folder and spells out step by step where to tap in Settings (Android 11 and up does not let apps install certificates themselves), then the config appears in the folder ready to select and connect. The certificate is generated per device and never leaves it — a shared certificate would let anyone holding it read everyone else's traffic, so that was deliberately not done. Two things to know: the final install confirmation has to be tapped by you on a system screen (Android grants no app that power), and the sites must be opened in a browser — the YouTube and Instagram apps do not work with this method.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "The local port now accepts both SOCKS and HTTP",
                            "The local port used to be SOCKS-only, so apps that only speak HTTP proxy couldn't use it. The same Local Port you see in Settings now accepts either.",
                            Icons.Default.SettingsEthernet
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Lower CPU and battery use from less core logging",
                            "The core's log level had been raised to debug an old issue and never lowered again. At that level the core wrote several lines per connection, and opening a single page is 50-150 connections -- constant CPU and storage use for the whole session, for information nobody reads in a release build.",
                            Icons.Default.BatteryChargingFull
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Lighter real-delay test",
                            "The delay test starts a fresh core per config (15 times in 6 seconds in one live log) and each was handed the full connection config: DNS servers, the fake-DNS pool, the whole routing table and a second local inbound -- none of which a single-URL measurement uses. Every test also took the same fixed local port, so concurrent tests all described the same port; each now gets its own. The scanner's health test and the Cloudflare scanner's delay test moved onto the same path. If the trimming ever fails, the previous full config is used so the test can't break because of it.",
                            Icons.Default.Timer
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Three hidden wastes removed from the connection config",
                            "All three were found in a live log from a real device, not by reading code. 1) Every DNS lookup ran twice: an IPv6 record was requested first, came back empty, and only then was the IPv4 record asked for -- measured at 150ms then 700ms for a single hostname, repeated for every domain any app opened. The config now follows the phone's own preference, so on IPv4-only mobile networks that first lookup disappears. 2) QUIC traffic (YouTube, TikTok, Instagram) was tunneled to a Cloudflare worker that carries no UDP at all, so every attempt stalled and died and the app retried over TCP anyway -- the only result was a few hundred milliseconds of delay before each video. It is now refused immediately so clients go straight to the path that works; game and voice traffic are untouched. 3) Traffic to Iran's block-page ranges was also being carried through the tunnel, spending full proxy latency to fetch a censorship notice; it is now blocked.",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Faster, more reliable connects by pinning the server IP",
                            "A config's server domain is now resolved by the app before the core starts and its IPs are pinned into the config, and the connection races several IPs at once, keeping whichever answers first. This matters most for Cloudflare-panel configs, which all point at a Cloudflare domain that resolves to dozens of different IPs, some of which are always slow or blocked from Iran. Previously the core tried those IPs one at a time, so a single bad IP meant seconds of stalling or a failed connect on a config that was perfectly fine. Resolution goes over DoH so it doesn't depend on the ISP's DNS, and if it fails for any reason the connection proceeds exactly as before. Result: lower delay and a higher connect success rate (download speed after connecting is unchanged).",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Two new built-in Iran configs (#7 and #8)",
                            "Taken from upstream Serverless-for-Iran v48 and shipped completely untouched. The six existing configs (based on v44) stay exactly as they are, so these are additions rather than a replacement. In v48, services that sanction Iran (GitHub, Microsoft, OpenAI, ...) are routed direct so they still see an Iranian IP instead of being broken by the evasion path; the DNS resolver itself is fronted through a clean Cloudflare domain; TLS traffic gets a dedicated two-stage fragment path; QUIC is blocked outright so YouTube/TikTok fall onto the more reliable path; the filter-page IP ranges are blackholed; and IPv6 is now preferred. #7 vs #8 differ only in the delay between fragments (#8 is for networks whose DPI objects to short gaps). Note: these configs require Local Port 10808.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Source review and public release on GitHub",
                            "The full Android app source is now published under GPLv3 on GitHub, so anyone can review it, contribute, or build their own version.",
                            Icons.Default.Code
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Automatic recovery after a real network drop",
                            "Previously, when Wi-Fi/mobile actually dropped, the tunnel had no way to notice, so the UI kept reporting \"connected\" indefinitely -- even after the network came back -- until the user manually disconnected and reconnected. The app now watches real connectivity and, if the network is lost while connected and later comes back, automatically cycles the same connection (stop, wait for full teardown, reconnect) instead of leaving it stuck.",
                            Icons.Default.Wifi
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Game tab: Server 1/2 replaced with the Aether engine",
                            "The two hardcoded legacy UAE WireGuard servers (one of which was already dead) were removed from the Game tab. Full-tunnel game boosting now uses the Aether engine, which picks the healthiest path across several protocols instead of a single fixed server.",
                            Icons.Default.SportsEsports
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the bottom nav bar's icons looking lopsided",
                            "Whenever the optional Game/WireGuard tabs made the two sides hold a different number of icons (e.g. 3 left / 2 right), the shorter side's icons visibly clustered toward the centre button with a dead gap past them, and ended up a different size than the other side's. Both sides now use a fixed, identical per-icon width and centre their (unpadded) icon group within their half -- whether Game and WireGuard are both on, one is, or neither is.",
                            Icons.Default.Apps
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed a dead empty gap under the hamburger drawer on tall phones",
                            "The drawer left an empty gap between the last menu item and the bottom of the screen on tall phones. The gap between the main menu and the emergency section now expands on tall screens (pinning the emergency section to the bottom), while still scrolling normally with everything packed together on short screens where it doesn't all fit.",
                            Icons.Default.Menu
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the local proxy passing no traffic in \"Proxy mode\"",
                            "In \"Proxy mode\" (local SOCKS/HTTP proxy only, no full-device VPN tunnel), connecting to a node could leave the app showing \"connected\" while the local proxy passed no traffic at all -- every site timed out through it. Root cause: proxy mode deliberately skips creating the VPN tunnel interface, but the Xray config was still being built as if a tunnel existed, handing xray-core a \"tun\" inbound pointing at an invalid file descriptor. xray-core accepted this at startup (reporting success), then the invalid inbound could crash the whole engine loop a few seconds later -- taking the SOCKS/HTTP proxy down with it, silently, after the UI had already committed to \"connected\". The tunnel inbound is now only included when a real VPN interface actually exists.",
                            Icons.Default.SettingsEthernet
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Aether: added a timer and explanation on the gateway-scan step",
                            "The gateway-scan step (MASQUE) can legitimately take anywhere from tens of seconds to a few minutes depending on scan mode, and on networks that DPI-block MASQUE it runs the full time budget before failing -- with only a static \"working\" badge, that read as a frozen app. The status card now shows a live elapsed-time counter on the scan step, and after 20 seconds an explanatory note that this step can take a while and that some networks block or slow MASQUE, with a reminder that tapping the connect button again cancels it. This doesn't change scan behavior or success rate -- it just makes a long scan legible instead of looking stuck.",
                            Icons.Default.Timer
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed invisible status bar icons in system light mode",
                            "The app's UI is always dark -- it never follows the system light/dark setting, its color scheme is hardcoded -- but edge-to-edge was letting the status/nav bar icon color follow that system setting anyway instead of what the app actually draws. On a phone with the system set to light mode, that gave dark clock/battery icons over the app's always-dark background: invisible (only worked if the phone itself was already in dark mode). Icon color is now forced to always match the app's own background, regardless of the phone's system theme.",
                            Icons.Default.PhoneAndroid
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Made scanner \"Real Delay\" and speed test results more accurate",
                            "This test is an actual Xray-proxied connection per config, not a raw ping -- but hundreds of configs were being tested with up to 8 real proxied connections running concurrently against the same servers, which reads as suspicious concurrent-handshake traffic to Cloudflare/DPI and gets throttled. The resulting delay/speed numbers were measuring self-inflicted congestion from the test itself, not real conditions. One of the two concurrency limits involved even carried a comment (\"Cloudflare/DPI might block if >3 concurrent handshakes\") directly contradicting the 8 it was actually set to. Both are now capped at 3, matching the safe value already used elsewhere in the app for the same kind of test.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Substantially reduced battery drain while connected",
                            "Four separate causes, all of which cost power continuously for as long as the VPN was up: 1) The tunnel held an unbounded partial wake lock for the entire session, so the CPU could never enter deep sleep or Doze while connected -- including overnight with the phone idle in a pocket and not a single byte moving. The lock is now held only while traffic is actually flowing and released after 5 minutes of silence; incoming packets still wake the process through the tunnel interface regardless. 2) A leftover diagnostic loop woke every 5 seconds for the whole session purely to write a RAM figure to the log that nothing reads in a release build -- roughly 17,000 wakeups a day doing no work; removed entirely. 3) The traffic meter wrote to storage every 2 seconds whether or not anything had changed -- about 43,000 pointless disk writes a day on an idle tunnel; it now only writes when bytes actually moved. 4) The auto-switch watcher re-read a single boolean every 10 seconds even though the feature is off by default; now checked once a minute.",
                            Icons.Default.BatteryChargingFull
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed redundant subscription-link uploads to Cloudflare",
                            "The background auto-sync re-uploaded a link's configs to Cloudflare on every node-list change, and most of those changes are metadata-only (a ping result, a speed-test figure, a country flag) that leave the actual config list byte-identical. Every one still cost a full Cloudflare API round trip, all day, for every link on every account -- wasted battery and data, and enough API traffic to plausibly contribute to the Cloudflare errors some users saw after leaving the app running for a day. Auto-sync now skips the upload entirely when the config list hasn't actually changed.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudSync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the cloud panel getting stuck behind \"email verification required\" for unrelated errors",
                            "Adding a Cloudflare account could get permanently stuck behind a \"verify your email\" overlay with no way out except an external Cloudflare login, even when email verification wasn't actually the problem. Root cause: when creating the account's workers.dev subdomain failed, the app decided it must be an unverified email purely by checking whether Cloudflare's raw error text happened to contain the word \"verify\" -- which several unrelated Cloudflare errors (token scope issues, rate limiting, etc.) can also contain, incorrectly locking the whole cloud panel behind the verification screen with no visibility into the real cause. It now asks Cloudflare's own account-status endpoint for the real verified state before showing that screen, and surfaces the actual error otherwise.",
                            Icons.Default.VerifiedUser
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed subscription-link configs going empty on their own",
                            "A subscription link's configs could silently go empty after a while, well before the user ever pressed \"Update\" -- any client would refresh the link and get zero configs back, even though the link had worked fine right after creation. Root cause: sub-links store which node group they serve as a composite string (e.g. \"manual:<engine>:<groupTitle>\"), and the screen's own \"Update\" button correctly parses that string apart to find the matching nodes -- but the background auto-sync job (which re-uploads a link's configs a few seconds after any node list change) was comparing a node's plain engine type directly against that whole composite string, which can never match. So every background sync silently overwrote the link with an empty config list; the \"after 20-30 refreshes\" users saw was really just how long it took one of those background syncs to fire. The auto-sync job now parses the group reference the same way the Update button does.",
                            Icons.Default.LinkOff
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "In-app update check, download and install",
                            "The app now checks GitHub for a newer release right after launch (and again whenever any VPN connection reaches \"connected\", in case GitHub itself was blocked at launch). If a newer version exists, a modal shows the new version number, download size, and the full scrollable changelog, with a \"Download new version\" button that opens a dedicated download screen (a fill progress bar with a live percentage) and hands off to the system installer automatically once the download completes. If GitHub is unreachable, the check fails completely silently with no interruption to the rest of the app.",
                            Icons.Default.SystemUpdate
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed misleading error when adding a Cloudflare account",
                            "Using a Global API Key without an email used to show a vague \"Invalid API Token\" error. It now clearly explains that this key type requires an email, with a hint added under the field too.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed \"Failed to upload MLM worker\" error",
                            "The MLM worker's compatibility date was computed from the phone's local clock; for timezones ahead of UTC (like Iran), between midnight and 3:30 AM local time this date landed a day ahead of Cloudflare's server date and the upload was rejected. It is now a fixed, safe date like every other worker in the app.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudUpload
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the hamburger drawer menu getting cut off on short screens",
                            "On some phones the emergency options at the bottom of the side menu were unreachable. The drawer content now scrolls fully on short screens.",
                            Icons.Default.Menu
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Updated the bundled BPB panel to v5.1.1",
                            "The BPB panel in the Cloud section was upgraded from the old v4.2.2 to v5.1.1. Since that version changed the deployment model entirely (per-account settings are now baked directly into the worker's own code instead of Cloudflare env variables like before), deploy, panel login, subscription fetching, and settings save were all rewritten for the new approach. The settings form was also updated to match the new version's fields.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudSync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Updated the bundled EDG worker to the latest build",
                            "The EDG worker in the Cloud section (based on cmliu/edgetunnel) was updated to the latest upstream release, carrying several months of transport optimizations (smarter up/downlink packet coalescing, concurrent TCP connection racing, faster DNS caching) that should make EDG configs noticeably faster and more stable. The swap is fully compatible -- no changes needed for existing accounts.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudSync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Updated the bundled Nahan panel to v3.0.0",
                            "The Nahan panel in the Cloud section was upgraded from v2.9.4 to v3.0.0: upstream VLESS proxy chaining, a v2rayN JSON subscription format, a redesigned add/edit-user form with modern toggle switches instead of checkboxes, and several RTL/dark-mode dashboard fixes. The D1 binding name and API route contract are unchanged, so the swap is fully compatible.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudSync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Removed the Deno panel entirely",
                            "The Deno panel (drawer entry, tab, FAQ section, and its underlying code) has been removed. api.deno.com has been blocked in Iran for a while now, and Deno's connection-duration-based billing model was never a good fit for VPN use anyway; the Cloudflare panels (BPB/EDG/Nahan) cover the same need without either problem.",
                            Icons.Default.Delete
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed MLM/Nahan deploys getting permanently stuck on a D1 database error",
                            "MLM (and Nahan) deploys could get permanently stuck failing at \"Failed to create D1 Database\", with no fix short of manually deleting old databases in the Cloudflare dashboard. Root cause: every deploy attempt -- including retries of a failed one -- provisioned a brand-new D1 database and never reused or cleaned up earlier ones, so a handful of retries would quietly eat into the account's D1 database quota until none was left to create. Deploys now reuse the account's existing database (or an orphaned one from a previous attempt) instead of always creating a new one. If the account's 10-database Free-plan limit is genuinely full of databases from other tools, the error now lists every existing database's name so you know exactly what to delete.",
                            Icons.Default.Storage
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Clarified the \"check domain\" button in the personal anti-sanction DNS",
                            "The check button only classifies a domain -- it never added it to the routing list on its own, but its \"sanctioned\" message read as if turning the feature on would be enough. Users would check a domain, see it's sanctioned, turn the feature on, and be confused when the site still didn't open because they never pressed the separate \"Add\" button. The message now says to press Add, and if a domain comes back sanctioned but isn't in your list yet, a red warning makes that explicit.",
                            Icons.Default.Warning
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed sanctioned sites (403 errors) still not opening in the personal anti-sanction DNS",
                            "Sanctioned sites -- confirmed \"sanctioned\" by the feature's own domain checker -- still didn't open, returning 403s exactly as if the feature were off. Root cause, found via a live logcat capture from a real device: domain routing relied entirely on sniffing the SNI out of the TLS ClientHello, which silently fails for any site using Encrypted Client Hello (ECH, increasingly Chrome's default) since the SNI is encrypted and invisible to sniffing -- every \"sanctioned\" connection fell through to the catch-all direct rule and hit the origin from the real Iranian IP. A fakedns pool fixed the resolution side, but a second live logcat capture showed the fake-IP-to-domain reverse lookup wasn't reliably matching the domain-based routing rule at routing time on this Xray build. Routing now matches directly on the fakedns pool's IP range (198.18.0.0/15) instead of depending on that reverse lookup at all.",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed unrelated sites breaking when the personal anti-sanction DNS was turned on",
                            "Turning this feature on could break sites that were never even supposed to be touched (e.g. Gmail). Root cause: its DNS resolver was hardcoded to plain UDP 1.1.1.1:53 -- a protocol commonly blocked or throttled on Iranian ISPs -- with no fallback, unlike the main VPN config which already resolves via DoH over HTTPS for exactly this reason. It now resolves via the same DoH-over-443 approach.",
                            Icons.Default.Dns
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "\"Update worker\" button in the personal anti-sanction DNS screen",
                            "Once an exit worker is already deployed, you can now redeploy the latest bundled EDG worker onto the same account without deleting and re-adding it.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudSync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the \"build worker\" button not updating after a successful deploy",
                            "After successfully deploying the exit worker in the personal anti-sanction DNS screen, it kept showing the \"build worker\" button instead of switching to \"ready\" -- you had to leave and re-enter the screen to see it had worked. Root cause: the deploy functions update the Cloudflare account object's fields in place, so the updated list pushed into the shared account state was structurally identical to what was already there, and Kotlin's StateFlow skips re-emitting a value it considers unchanged. Now tracks deploy success locally instead.",
                            Icons.Default.Refresh
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the anti-sanction DNS worker deploy failing permanently on retries",
                            "This step (the same EDG worker deploy used elsewhere) could fail permanently after a few retries for the same reason MLM/Nahan did: it always created a brand-new KV namespace and never reused or cleaned up earlier ones. It now reuses the account's existing namespace (or an orphaned one from a previous attempt); failures now show the real Cloudflare error too.",
                            Icons.Default.CloudSync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the Scanner tab's \"Ping Health Test\" button hanging or crashing",
                            "This button on combined/mixed config groups (and the MLM \"update subscription with healthy IPs\" flow that depends on it) could hang indefinitely or crash the app on some devices. Root cause: this specific code path was missing the native Xray core initialization call that every other testing feature in the app performs first -- one line was accidentally commented out. Whether it worked depended entirely on some other screen having already initialized the core earlier in the same app session, which is why it worked reliably for some users/flows and not others. Restored the missing call.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the MLM \"User Management\" screen getting stuck on \"Error fetching users\"",
                            "This screen could get permanently stuck on that error right after a fresh deploy. Root cause: Cloudflare's own edge returns a transient 404 (its own error code 1042) for the first few seconds after a brand-new *.workers.dev route is enabled, before the worker code even runs. The app now retries a few times automatically; any other real failure now shows the actual HTTP status/response instead of a generic message.",
                            Icons.Default.Refresh
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "New \"Get free configs\" button in the Connection tab",
                            "A multi-step wizard above the + button: it first shows how many configs are available across a few public sources, you pick how many you want, and the system tests them in two stages (a fast reachability filter, then a real Xray-proxied connection test -- the same accuracy as \"Real Delay\") and only keeps genuinely working ones. A \"this many is enough\" button lets you stop the search early and keep what's found so far. Configs land under a \"Free Configs\" folder in the Manual tab with random mlmvpnXXXX names, and anything you already have is skipped instead of duplicated (it tells you how many were already yours).",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Real per-config country flags, no full connection required",
                            "During the \"Real Delay\" test, if a config connects and its flag isn't known yet, the app queries the config's real exit country through that same live tunnel (once -- cached afterward, no repeat lookups) and shows it on the card. No need to fully connect anymore just to see a config's real flag.",
                            Icons.Default.Flag
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed a mismatched flag above the Connect button",
                            "The flag above the Connect button came from Cloudflare's own geoIP database (the `loc` field in cdn-cgi/trace), which occasionally disagreed with reality (e.g. showing Canada for a US exit IP). It now uses the same source as the per-config flags, so both always agree with each other and with sites like ip.me.",
                            Icons.Default.Sync
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed noticeable lag and slowness on the Aether tunnel",
                            "The tunnel adapter used the app-wide MTU of 1420, a value chosen for VLESS/TCP outbounds. MASQUE is UDP: every inner packet is wrapped in QUIC, and once the QUIC short header, the encryption tag, the datagram frame header and the outer UDP and IP headers are counted, that adds roughly 80 bytes -- putting a full-size packet exactly at the 1500-byte Ethernet ceiling with no headroom at all. QUIC sets the \"do not fragment\" bit, so on any path with a smaller MTU (such as the 1492 typical of ADSL/VDSL lines here) every large packet was dropped outright instead of being split. Small requests still worked while large transfers spent their time retransmitting -- exactly the \"connects fine but lags\" behaviour. Aether now uses a dedicated MTU of 1280, the IPv6 minimum, which is deliverable on any path.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the tunnel wrongly staying \"connected\" after it died",
                            "The connected flag was one-way: once set, no later stage could clear it. A tunnel that dropped and fell back to scanning kept the UI green and kept routing traffic at a port with nothing behind it -- the app reported success while carrying nothing. Any stage other than connected now explicitly means the data path is gone.",
                            Icons.Default.LinkOff
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed getting stuck on the \"validating data flow\" step",
                            "The engine writes its log from several independent parts, so \"tunnel validated\" sometimes arrived a few milliseconds after \"proxy ready\" even though it happened first. The UI read that as going backwards, dropped from connected to validating, and stayed there -- on a tunnel that was in fact perfectly healthy. Steps can now only move backwards on an explicit disconnect signal, never on a late progress message.",
                            Icons.Default.Timeline
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Game tab: no more fake \"Boosted\"",
                            "The boost button announced success the moment the service was asked to start. That is accurate for the other modes, but Aether is only beginning to enrol an identity and hunt for a gateway at that point, which takes anywhere from a few seconds to two minutes -- so the button went green while traffic was still leaving untouched. The real connection steps are now shown live, and \"Boosted\" appears only once the tunnel is actually up.",
                            Icons.Default.SportsEsports
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Game tab: fixed the first tap on Boost doing nothing",
                            "The first tap appeared to do nothing yet started the engine, and the second tap warned that a VPN was already running. Two causes: the status watcher ran immediately on tap while the engine only comes up about a third of a second later, so it read the leftover value from the previous session (\"stopped\") and declared the boost failed on the spot. On top of that the button accepted taps in every state, so the second tap found the engine it had itself started one tap earlier. The watcher now waits for this session's engine to actually come up, and the button is disabled while connecting.",
                            Icons.Default.TouchApp
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Game tab: live ping and baseline ping now report correctly",
                            "A game boost puts only the selected game inside the tunnel and deliberately leaves the app itself outside, but the live ping was measured directly -- over the raw internet path, the opposite of what the card claims to show. On filtered lines that path usually did not answer at all, so the reading sat on \"measuring\". It is now measured through the tunnel itself. The baseline (\"on your own internet\") was also never measured for Aether and always read \"unknown\"; it is now taken just before the tunnel comes up.",
                            Icons.Default.NetworkPing
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Game tab: protocol and scan mode selection, and game-only routing",
                            "You can now choose between MASQUE and WireGuard, and between turbo and balanced scanning. The game profile is tuned for latency: HTTP/3 over QUIC is enforced (a game's UDP inside a TCP tunnel suffers head-of-line blocking), obfuscation is kept light to reduce per-packet overhead, and the keepalive is short so a carrier NAT cannot drop the mapping mid-match. More importantly, a boost no longer tunnels the whole device -- only the selected game goes through.",
                            Icons.Default.Tune
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Removed \"UAE DNS\" from the Game tab",
                            "The UAE server has been decommissioned, so the option could do nothing except add a pointless delay to every automatic run. It is gone from the mode list and from the automatic selection race.",
                            Icons.Default.DeleteSweep
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Clearer message when clearing identities",
                            "The old message called every file an identity, so a single connection reported \"2 files\" and looked as though the app had created two accounts. In reality each protocol keeps a small record of its last working server alongside its identity, and that record is not an account. The message now counts the two separately.",
                            Icons.Default.Info
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "WireGuard is back in the Aether protocol list",
                            "The mode had been hidden on the belief that the cause of its repeated disconnects could not be investigated from within this project. That belief was wrong -- the relevant source is present in this repository. Three real faults were fixed: the tunnel's disconnect messages were not recognised by the step list (so the whole drop-and-reconnect cycle was invisible), the obfuscation profile chosen by the user had no effect on this protocol because a name the relevant part does not recognise was being sent to the engine, and the \"guaranteed\" scan mode built for exactly this situation was never offered at all.",
                            Icons.Default.VpnKey
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Stopped the Aether screen interfering with a game boost",
                            "The engine is shared across the app, so the Aether screen's automatic recovery also saw sessions started by the Game tab. That recovery can clear the identity and reconnect -- which, run in the middle of a game boost, would destroy it. Each screen now only recovers a session it started itself.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Detailed connection reporting for troubleshooting",
                            "A live report was added that records the settings actually sent to the engine, the current step, the selected server, and whether the local proxy is genuinely reachable. The two states that were hardest to identify from the outside are now named explicitly: when the status says connected but the local proxy does not answer, and when a scan ends with every server having failed.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        )
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.2.0",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the Google Script crash",
                            "Until now, a few seconds after you disconnected Google Script, the app would close on its own and start again from scratch. The cause was the tunnel's internal core, which took the whole app down with it as it shut off. That core now runs separately from the app, so if it runs into trouble while shutting down, only it ends — your app, your connection and your settings stay exactly where they were. Disconnecting is instant from now on, with no more closing and reopening.",
                            Icons.Default.BugReport
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "GATE MLMVPN — a new connection engine (new)",
                            "A completely new way to connect, behind the big button in the middle of the bottom bar. It runs over TCP on port 443, so it works even on lines where UDP is restricted. Thousands of servers worldwide with country flags, and every server seen so far is kept in an archive — not just the ones live at this moment.",
                            Icons.Default.Public
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "A real connection test, separate from ping",
                            "Ping only tells you how long a packet takes to arrive, not whether a connection will succeed. The real test performs a full handshake with the server, so a green result means that server actually connects on your line. The list also sorts itself by the fastest verified server.",
                            Icons.Default.VerifiedUser
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Browse by continent and country",
                            "Pick several countries at once, test them all in one go, add the healthy ones to your main list and prune the dead ones. A full guide next to the close button explains every feature.",
                            Icons.Default.TravelExplore
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Optional UDP acceleration",
                            "Speeds things up on networks where UDP is open. Since it doesn't help everywhere, it's left as a switch under your control.",
                            Icons.Default.Speed
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Privacy: server IPs and ports are no longer shown",
                            "Addresses and ports were removed from the server cards so a screenshot can't give them away. You can still sort by those values.",
                            Icons.Default.Security
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "WireGuard tab fully redesigned",
                            "A big connect button in the middle, the settings that matter right beneath it, everything else on its own screen, and the connection status at the bottom. What used to be a crowded, confusing screen now reads at a glance.",
                            Icons.Default.Security
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "WireGuard: fixed the constant reconnect loop",
                            "A healthy tunnel was being declared dead after about 12 seconds and restarted from scratch — an idle tunnel was indistinguishable from a broken one, and the first request never had time to finish. That window was corrected and the connection now holds.",
                            Icons.Default.CheckCircle
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "WireGuard: automatic connection recovery",
                            "If the first transport doesn't answer, the other one is tried automatically, and if the WARP identity has been rejected a fresh one is enrolled — with nothing for you to do.",
                            Icons.Default.Autorenew
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Startup animation",
                            "The MLMVPN logo now turns into a rocket and lifts off. The main screen loads behind the animation, so none of that time is wasted.",
                            Icons.Default.Star
                        )
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.1.0",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Personal anti-sanction DNS (new)",
                            "A new menu section that opens only sanctioned sites (ChatGPT, Gemini, GitHub, Steam…) through your own Cloudflare worker with a clean IP, while everything else stays direct. You can test any domain and add it to the list.",
                            Icons.Default.Shield
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Emergency #2 (Google Apps Script) redesigned",
                            "The auto-deploy flow is replaced by a step-by-step wizard: choose a password, confirm it, get the ready-to-paste code with a deployment guide and a direct link, enter the Deployment ID, and test the relay. Multiple Google accounts are supported for load-balancing and bypassing quotas.",
                            Icons.Default.Cloud
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the script copy issue",
                            "The copied code is now completely clean with no stray characters (BOM and comments stripped), plus a 'Save as file' option for when the phone clipboard truncates it.",
                            Icons.Default.ContentCopy
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Install the CA certificate without connecting first",
                            "You no longer need to connect once beforehand — the CA can be generated and installed straight from the card at the top of the Emergency #2 screen.",
                            Icons.Default.Security
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed the app closing on Emergency #2 disconnect",
                            "After disconnecting, instead of abruptly closing to the launcher, the app now restarts cleanly in a controlled way.",
                            Icons.Default.CheckCircle
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Improved phone back-button behavior",
                            "Back now behaves like a standard app: returns to the home (Cloud) tab from any screen, goes up one level in two-level screens, and asks to confirm exit on the home tab.",
                            Icons.Default.ArrowBack
                        )
                    )
                ),
                ChangelogVersion(
                    "Version 1.0.9 — Emergency Edition",
                    listOf(
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Built for harsh conditions & national internet",
                            "This release is specially tuned for optimal use under harsh network conditions and the national internet, so you can still create servers, manage them, and connect even during heavy outages and filtering.",
                            Icons.Default.Bolt
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Emergency #2 (Google Apps Script) fully optimized",
                            "The Google Apps Script based Emergency #2 infrastructure has been fully optimized for a more stable and faster connection under harsh network conditions.",
                            Icons.Default.Cloud
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Emergency #1 (Vercel) now covers all 4 panels",
                            "The Emergency #1 rescue route now works for all 4 panels (MLM, Nahan, BPB, Edge): deploy, settings, user management and fetching configs go through Vercel even when direct access to Cloudflare is blocked.",
                            Icons.Default.Cloud
                        ),
                        ChangelogItem(
                            "Fixed cloud config (xHTTP) lag",
